101.\par
We Can Run Away Together.\par
\par
\par
Normally, as soon as his head hit the pillow, Bill fell asleep and didn\rquote t wake up until morning. This time, however, he was awoken in the middle of the night by a nightmare. He had dreamt that he had lost Layla. He\par
felt so disturbed by the bad dream, he didn\rquote t want to think about it.\par
\ldblquote What\rquote s a horrible dream like that doing in my head?\rdblquote  he grumbled, sitting up in bed.\par
As he stared angrily into the darkness, he cast an even darker shadow on the floor.\par
He lay down again, hoping to get back to sleep, but immediately his head started swimming with unpleasant memories of the nightmare.\par
He didn\rquote t understand where the bad thoughts were coming from. He and Layla were going to live a happy lite i n the capital, where she was going to go to college, thanks to the support of the House of Herhardt. Of course, he was a\par
\par
little concerned about the fact that she and Kyle would be attending the same university. However, whether the two of them ended up getting back together or not, he was ready to accept her decision. With nothing but good things on the horizon for her, why had he had a bad dream about her?\par
Feeling very angry at the nightmare, he sat up in bed. He felt ready to pour a basin of ice water over his head, if it would clear his minds of these thoughts. Throwing off the covers, he headed to the kitchen to get a glass of water. Before he could turn on the faucet, however, he froze at the sight of something he saw through the window.\par
There was a slender young woman dressed in white walking towards the cottage on the forest path. Having just had a nightmare, he wondered if he was seeing things. Just as he was beginning to worry if there was something wrong with his brain, he recognized the woman.\par
\ldblquote Layla\'85?\rdblquote\par
The woman walking through the dark forest like a ghost was unmistakably Layla. However, she looked extremely tired. Her face bore the expression of a sad, tired old woman. What was going on with her?\par
He felt like running outside to see if it was really her, then he changed his mind and hurried back to bed. Shortly thereafter he heard the front door open, followed by the sound of careful, quiet steps. Not wanting to believe that it was her, he stayed in bed.\par
It was just a ghost, he told himself, practically wishing that it was true. It wasn\rquote t her, it was just a ghost.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
As if his wish had been granted, the Layla he saw at breakfast that morning looked like her usual self Having already gotten ready for work, she was busy preparing the food. Once everything was on the table, she sat down across from him, smiling. She looked brighter and livelier than an early-spring morning.\par
\ldblquote Is something on your mind, Uncle Bill?\rdblquote  she asked, noticing the unusual look on his face.\par
\ldblquote Huh? N-no,\rdblquote  he stammered, waving his hands in the air. \ldblquote Why would I have something on my mind? Nope, I sure don\rquote t\'85\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Okay\'85\rdblquote  she replied, tilting her head as she served him some eggs and a piece of bread. His obstinate denial made her feel even more certain that something was wrong. \ldblquote You really look like you have something to say. Did something happen?\rdblquote\par
He shook his head adamantly. \ldblquote What are you talking about?! Nothing happened.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Are you feeling well\'85?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Stop treating me like an old geezer. I\rquote m still as strong and healthy as I ever was. I could lift you over my head right now. Want me to show you?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No, no, you don\rquote t have to do that,\rdblquote  she replied, giggling. \ldblquote I\rquote m glad to hear you\rquote re feeling well, though.\rdblquote\par
The sight of her smiling face was so lovely, he felt a tinge of pain in his otherwise calm heart. It was only after he had adopted the young Layla that he\par
\par
had come to realize that deep love always included a certain amount of sadness.\par
\ldblquote You should eat up, or soon you\rquote ll be as skinny as a rail again,\rdblquote  he said, spreading butter thickly on a piece of bread and holding it out to her.\par
As always, she took the food he was offering her with a gentle smile and obediently took a bite of it. Somehow, seeing her do this made him want to cry.\par
I really am getting old, he wistfully thought. He had already been getting on in years when she had shown up at his cottage as a little girl. Now she was all grown up.\par
He tucked into his breakfast, and by the time he had cleared his plate, his feelings of weakness had passed. He knew that he had to stay hearty and healthy for Layla\rquote s sake. He needed to be around to see her grow wings and fly off to success, and to stop any bad guys from snatching her away. One day she was going to give birth to babies that looked just like her. He could only imagine how cute those grandchildren would be\'85\par
Suddenly he realized that he had grown very sentimental. He shook his head once again to clear his mind both of the sentimentality and of the lingering bad feelings from the nightmare.\par
After breakfast, instead of going out early to the garden like usual, he waited around so that he could see Layla off. She seemed moved by this simple gesture. As she left, she kept looking over her shoulder and waving. TH see you after work!\rdblquote  she shouted from amid the fresh smell of the early-spring plants.\par
He watched for a long time as she got on her bicycle and rode off into the distance. When we move to Ratse, I should buy her a new bike, he thought. Just then, he felt another pang in his heart. He ran his hands over his face, then\par
\par
walked over to the wheelbarrow and started loading it up with tools. I\rquote m turning into a crybaby in my old age, he thought.\par
