102.\par
\par
A Completely Different Direction.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
Starting early in the morning, the servants were busy packing up Katharina and Elise von Herhardt\rquote s luggage.\par
Every year in early spring, the empire\rquote s most prominent noblewomen gathered in the capital to participate in the empress\rquote s birthday celebrations. It was the unofficial start of the social season, so all families that were invited took great pains to prepare for the festivities.\par
Hessen quietly approached Dowager and Lady Herhardt and said, \ldblquote The carriage is ready for departure.\rdblquote\par
Smiling elegantly, the two former duchesses rose from their seats and started walking. Their maids followed quietly behind. Meeting them at the top of the grand staircase that led to the mansion\rquote s main hall, Matthias escorted his grandmother down the stairs.\par
\par
\ldblquote It would have been nice if you could have departed with us,\rdblquote  Katharina said, looking at her grandson.\par
Due to his packed schedule, Matthias wasn\rquote t going to be able to make it to the capital until the day of the birthday party.\par
\ldblquote I know you\rquote re busy, but you mustn\rquote t let business take up all your time and energy. Your social life matters, too. You know better than anyone how important it is for the head of the household to show his face at social events whenever possible,\rdblquote  she said in a slightly worried tone.\par
While it was true that the world was changing and aristocrats increasingly had to rely on business to make their living, this only made one\rquote s position and reputation in social circles all the more important. While capitalists without aristocratic titles were mingling more and more with aristocrats, this had actually made the invisible wall between the two groups even higher and stronger.\par
\ldblquote Yes, Grandmother,\rdblquote  Matthias replied, smiling politely.\par
While Elise and Katharina were relieved to see him acting like his usual self, some uneasiness remained in their eyes as they exchanged glances.\par
\ldblquote See you in Ratse, Matthias,\rdblquote  Elise said, hugging her son briefly before climbing into the carriage.\par
Matthias maintained perfect decorum as he saw them off.\par
\ldblquote Mr. Stauf will be coming to Arvis this afternoon, Your Grace,\rdblquote  Hessen quietly told Matthias as he followed him back into the main hall.\par
Matthias nodded to show that he was listening.\par
The Stauf family had managed the House of Herhardt\rquote s Internal legal Issues for generations.\par
\ldblquote Mr. Stauf also said that if you would like more detailed advice about the matter, you may want to bring the company\rquote s legal counsel into the discussion\par
\par
as well.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote ll decide about that after today\rquote s meeting.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes, Your Grace. I\rquote ll relay that to Mr. Stauf.\rdblquote  Hessen then walked off towards the hallway.\par
Matthias stopped in the middle of the large, marble-floored hall and slowly raised his head. His blue eyes looked coldly at the large chandelier, and at the Herhardt family crest imprinted on the ceiling.\par
Lowering his gaze, he continued walking across the hall with a more relaxed gait.\par
\ldblquote Elise, do you really think that\rquote s what\rquote s going on?\rdblquote\par
Elise and Katharina\rquote s eyes filled with worry as soon as the carriage had left the estate. Looking tired, Elise removed her gloves. \ldblquote It\rquote s hard to believe that Matthias of all people would do that, but we mustn\rquote t forget that he\rquote s a man. On the face of it, it\rquote s hard to understand the kindness he\rquote s showing to Bill Remmer. But with Layla Llewellyn added to the picture, it all makes sense. Don\rquote t you agree?\rdblquote\par
Katharina looked out the carriage window, neither agreeing nor disagreeing. She had been a highly capable and influential duchess in her day, as had her daughter-in-law. She knew it wasn\rquote t worth raising a fuss over things like this.\par
\ldblquote Why not just ask him about it after he arrives in Ratse?\rdblquote  she asked Elise.\par
\ldblquote I thought about that, but I don\rquote t think it\rquote s a good idea,\rquote  Elise replied. \ldblquote Matthias seemed like he was waiting for us to ask him. Surely you know what it would mean if he has made up his mind that way.\rdblquote\par
Dowager Herhardt understood her daughter-in-law\rquote s point at once. If their surmise proved true, the situation was a thorny one.\par
\par
There was clearly something going on between Matthias and the gardener\rquote s adopted daughter. They had already suspected that this was the case on the afternoon when Matthias had informed them of his unexpected decision to relocate the gardener and his daughter to Ratse. Now it was no longer a matter of merely suspecting. Matthias was being open about their relationship, while his mother and grandmother were assiduously feigning ignorance.\par
\ldblquote If we\rquote re right about Matthias, what can we do to protect the family?\rdblquote  Elise asked.\par
\ldblquote I think the best thing to do is to continue to feign ignorance, Elise,\rdblquote  Katharina replied, whispering even though she knew that no one was listening in on their conversation.\par
Matthias and Claudine\rquote s wedding was right around the corner. Ensuring that the ceremony was not marred by any incidents or scandals was of prime importance.\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t you think that\rquote s for the best, though? The fact that Matthias wants to send Layla Llewellyn to the capital shows that he has no intention of letting his marriage be ruined.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Sure, I suppose the most important thing right now is making sure that the wedding goes off without a hitch\rquote\par
Elise felt like sighing, but she stopped herself and just nodded. If it were up to her, she would question Matthias. Knowing how much time and effort both families had put into the preparations for the wedding, she would see to it that Layla was removed from the situation before she could get in the way of the wedding. However, she knew that trying to do this would only make things worse. Thus, the best way to avoid ruining the wedding seemed to be simply to ignore Layla.\par
\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m a little afraid, though,\rdblquote  she muttered, rubbing her temples. \ldblquote Matthias seems like he\rquote s trying to get caught. What if by some chance this leads to his engagement getting broken off, and then he declares his intention to, God forbid, marry Layla Llewellyn?\rdblquote\par
Katharina, who was as proud as could be of her grandson, frowned at her daughter-in-law\rquote s words. \ldblquote Do you really think our Matthias would do such a thing?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m worried precisely because it\rquote s Matthias,\rdblquote  Elise replied. The veins in her pale hands were starting to bulge.\par
Matthias had been an extremely well-behaved child. He had always fulfilled his duties perfectly and had never talked back. However, Elise had never felt that her son acted this way out of obedience to her or anyone else.\par
He had behaved himself out of a desire to become the perfect successor to the House of Herhardt. If his desire were to shift, then he was liable to stop acting like a perfect successor at the drop of a hat. He always behaved with flawless decorum, but at the same time, he was arrogant and firm-willed. She knew he wouldn\rquote t show obedience to anyone if doing so meant going against his own desire.\par
\ldblquote But I suppose you\rquote re right,\rdblquote  she said, trying to suppress the ominous feeling inside her, \ldblquote he probably wouldn\rquote t do such a thing. It\rquote s Matthias, after all.\rdblquote\par
