After standing nearby for a while, waiting for their chance, two young ladies hurried up to Claudine. \ldblquote Hello, Lady Claudine,\rdblquote  one of them said.\par
\ldblquote We met a long time ago at a party at the Herhardt mansion. Do you remember?\rdblquote\par
The other lady shook her head and said, \ldblquote It was such a brief meeting, she probably doesn\rquote t remember us. Let\rquote s introduce ourselves. My name is-\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Of course I remember you. Lady Escher,\rquote  Claudine said, gently cutting the young lady off.\par
Escher started blushing immediately.\par
\ldblquote And I remember you as well, Lady Diane.\rdblquote\par
The other lady, the shorter of the two, started blushing as well.\par
The sisters were practically jumping up and down with excitement. Their mother, a viscountess, was frowning at them from a few paces away, but this\par
\par
didn\rquote t stop the immature girls.\par
They showered Claudine with an endless stream of praise and told her how much they were looking forward to her wedding.\par
Claudine had already chatted with dozens of other young ladies who had said much the same things to her, but she still reacted as if they were saying the most fascinating things in the world to her. This was one of the rules of etiquette that she had learned early in life, and by now it was second nature to her. She had also learned to memorize the name of everyone she met at social events.\par
After chatting with Escher and Diane, she met countless more noblewomen and young ladies desperate to form an acquaintance with her.\par
The party proceeded in this manner. There was the usual conversation and laughter, with beautiful music playing in the background, and a lovely floral scent in the air. It was a perfect soiree.\par
After chatting with every other important noblewoman, Claudine concluded her rounds by exchanging pleasantries with the crown princess. After that, she went to the lounge and sat on the sofa by the window for some well-earned rest.\par
Countess Brandt, who had been watching her daughter from afar, walked up to her, smiling brightly. \ldblquote Since the duke\rquote s mother and grandmother couldn\rquote t attend, you\rquote re the social queen of the empire tonight, Claudine.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Mother\'85\rdblquote  Claudine said, frowning at her mother.\par
Countess Brandt wasn\rquote t going to stop, however. \ldblquote Hey, It\rquote s true!\rdblquote  she said, her eyes full of pride In her beloved daughter.\par
While a family\rquote s power was the husband\rquote s domain, it fell to the lady of the house to maintain a dignified reputation commensurate with that power. No matter how high-ranking or wealthy a family was. if the lady of the house\par
\par
wasn\rquote t well-respected in high society, the family would never rise above a middling level of prestige.\par
This was how the House of Herhardt had established itself at the top of aristocratic society. While the family\rquote s long, honorable history had given it the authority to dominate the aristocracy, each generation\rquote s Duchess Herhardt had been treated like the queen of high society. No one could deny the roles these duchesses had played in maintaining the internal stability of the family.\par
