A nightingale\rquote s full-throated song rang out from the dark forest. From somewhere in the distance, an owl hooted in response.\par
The night breeze ruffled Bill\rquote s hair. He brushed it back with a stiff hand. He stared at the riverside cabin in front of him, but dared not take another step towards it. He could see that the lights were on inside.\par
He had impulsively decided to follow Layla after he had seen the ghostly image of her walking outside again. He had wondered with some concern If she might have been sleepwalking.\par
While he was following her, she had suddenly disappeared from his sight. He had hurried out of the woods, but she had been nowhere to be seen. He had looked carefully along the empty riverbank, and the river flowing beyond it, before discovering the dark cabin.\par
At first, he hadn\rquote t believed his eyes, thinking that the disgraceful letter was causing him to see things. Just as he was about to turn back, however, he had seen a man walking towards the cabin from the other direction. Bill had then quickly hidden behind a tree. It hadn\rquote t taken long for him to recognize who the man was. It was the duke.\par
He had continued to watch as Duke Herhardt calmly climbed the stairs, entered the cabin, and turned on the lights inside. Bill had breathed a sigh of relief, concluding that, since the lights hadn\rquote t been on, Layla must not be in the cabin. However, a lingering doubt deep in his heart kept him from being able to leave.\par
\par
\ldblquote Pull yourself together, Bill,\rdblquote  he muttered to himself, shaking his head.\par
For all he knew, Layla had simply gone out for a nighttime stroll. Maybe she was already back in her bed in the cottage, sound asleep.\par
Just as he was finally about to turn back, he heard the sound of a door opening and saw the duke walk out onto the cabin\rquote s balcony. There was a woman there as well.\par
Bill dreaded to think who it was.\par
\par
