As Layla walked down the stairs on the outside of the cabin, her face was expressionless and as pale as the moonlight. There was no trace of the warm smile she had shown the duke before kissing him.\par
As she reached the bottom of the staircase, she adjusted her glasses and took a moment to catch her breath. Then she started briskly walking, as if there was nothing wrong.\par
It was a night like any other night. She had experienced so many similar nights since the winter, she didn\rquote t feel especially hurt or shaken by this one. Instead, she felt lighthearted and relieved at the thought that this long procession of nights would soon be coming to an end. Reflecting on this, she started smiling cheerfully. Her steps also grew lighter, until she was frolicking through the woods like she had loved to do as a little girl.\par
See? You\rquote re doing a good job, she thought to herself, smiling proudly. Just then, however, she felt something warm running down her cheek. Confused, she blinked rapidly, and then she felt the same sensation again and again. Only\par
\par
once her vision grew cloudy did she realize that tears were falling from her eyes.\par
She picked up her pace, but the tears wouldn\rquote t stop. Unsure of what was going on, she laughed even as she continued to cry. She thought back to the snowy winter night when she had decided to hate the duke. If she had known how cumbersome it was to harbor hate, she might have reconsidered her decision. It felt like a hot lump of metal was pressing down on her heart. She wanted to let go of the hate, but she wasn\rquote t sure how to do it.\par
Perhaps the hate would vanish once she left Arvis? She prayed that this would be the case. When it came to things related to the duke, she didn\rquote t even want to hold onto her hatred. If it were only possible, she would have liked to have all her memories of him wiped from her mind, so that she wouldn\rquote t even know who he was anymore.\par
As she turned from the riverside onto the forest path, her stifled tears gave way to sobbing. Her fierce hatred for the duke made it impossible to stop crying. To her tear-filled eyes, the forest path looked like an endless maze.\par
Just then, amid her sobs, she heard a shaky voice call out to her. \ldblquote Layla!\rdblquote\par
Without even thinking to wipe her tears away, she turned in the direction of the voice. She was sure it had been her imagination. It had to have been.\par
Her eyes went wide. \ldblquote Uncle Bill\'85?\rdblquote\par
Bill, who normally stood tall and sturdy as a tree, was walking towards her with a hesitant, wavering gait. Still wondering it she was Imagining things, she blinked several times and wiped the tears from her eyes, but she still saw him in front of her. Soon his shadow reached her feet.\par
She winced at him, her face flushed red, and then her legs gave out and she collapsed to the ground.\par
