109.\par
\par
Final Wish.\par
\par
\par
The morning sun was shining brightly, but it was still quiet in the cottage.\par
Layla\rquote s tears had dried. She was staring blankly at the sun\rquote s glare reflected in the surface of the old kitchen table.\par
As she slowly closed and opened her unfocused eyes, her mind kept flashing through disjointed memories from the previous night. She saw Uncle Bill standing in the woods, crying. She heard the flapping wings of the birds whose slumber had been disturbed by his sobs. She felt the tears that had run down her cheeks as she clutched at his coat, begging him to stop after he declared that he was going to go find the duke and kill him. All these fractured memories seemed strange and alien to her now.\par
Finally, Bill\rquote s hoarse voice broke the silence. \ldblquote You can\rquote t continue doing this, Layla. Something bad must have happened\'85 something very bad. Otherwise, you never would\rquote ve done this, I\rquote m sure of it.\rdblquote\par
He felt like his heart was being torn apart as he remembered Layla as a little girl arriving one day in the mail coach, an upright, good little girl who grew up to be prettier than any flower he had grown in his whole life.\par
\par
I\rquote d rather die than see her living life as the duke\rquote s mistress, he thought. But no, I can\rquote t die. First I have to kill him.\par
\ldblquote Tell me, Layla. Tell me what the duke is holding over your head.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Uncle Bill\'85\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote And don\rquote t even think about lying to me. I know that you would sooner die than voluntarily engage in that sort of behavior.\rdblquote\par
Her face stained with deep fear and despair, Layla looked less like someone blinded by ignoble love, and more like an animal cornered by a hunter.\par
\ldblquote Is he threatening you? Is he blackmailing you? What could he possibly\'85\rdblquote  Bill suddenly fell silent as a horrible thought crossed his mind. The shift in\par
Layla\rquote s behavior had occurred in the winter, after his accident. More specifically, it was right around when the duke had dropped the charges against him, a display of generosity far beyond what anyone would have expected an aristocrat to show to a member of his staff.\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t tell me\'85\rdblquote\par
The color started to drain from Bill\rquote s face. Could it be that it wasn\rquote t merely the duke\rquote s extraordinary generosity that had saved Bill from prison? Could the duke have demanded something in return? And what about the duke\rquote s decision to let Bill keep his job? What about his offer to sponsor Layla\rquote s college education? Could it all have been part of a dirty, shameful dead.\par
\ldblquote N-no\rquote\ldblquote  Layla shouted, shaking her head. \ldblquote It\rquote s not like that. Not at all, Uncle Bill!\rdblquote  She bolted up from her seat and kneeled down in front of him. \ldblquote It\rquote s all my fault. I\rquote ve been doing bad things out of my own greed. I\rquote m sorry, Uncle Bill. I did wrong. It\rquote s all my fault.\rquote\par
She\rquote s lying, he thought. Before this thought entered his mind, he had already felt the pain of it in his heart.\par
\par
He wanted to bite his tongue as he remembered believing that all the good fortune they had met had been because of the amazing kindness of that noble gentleman, Duke Herhardt.\par
\ldblquote Not only did I only get out of jail because you traded your body for my freedom, but I called that scoundrel my savior\'85!\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No. It\rquote s not like that, Uncle Bill. Not at all. No!\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I won\rquote t let this stand! I\rquote m going to kill that bastard myself!\rdblquote\par
He rose from his seat full of rage, but was stopped short as another thought occurred to him. If he did anything to the duke, it would ruin Layla\rquote s reputation. In the court of public opinion, if it came down to a contest between Duke Herhardt and Layla, it was obvious who everyone would side with.\par
\ldblquote Please, Uncle Bill, please don\rquote t! I couldn\rquote t live with myself if you went back to jail. Okay?\rdblquote  She moved closer to him and grabbed his hands. \lquote I\rquote m\'85 I\rquote m going to end it all. I\rquote m going to run away. Before he\rquote s married. I\rquote m going to be far, far away. I\rquote m never going to see him again. I promise.\rdblquote\par
Only then did he understand why she had been so stubborn about not wanting to accept the Herhardt family\rquote s sponsorship, and why she had proposed that they move to a distant city. It had been a cry for help that he had completely missed, blinded by his admiration for what he believed was the duke\rquote s kindness.\par
\ldblquote Layla! You poor thing!\rdblquote\par
He burst into tears and flopped back down onto his chair. Indifferent to his sadness, the morning sunlight continued to shine with a harsh intensity.\par
