\ldblquote Miss Llewellyn, are you feeling sick?\rdblquote  Monica asked, looking worriedly at her princess teacher.\par
\ldblquote Miss Llewellyn\'85?!\rdblquote  As her teacher continued not to respond, Monica\rquote s eyes started to fill with tears.\par
\par
Layla kept staring blankly at her desk until Monica reached out and shook her arm. \ldblquote Oh\'85 I\rquote m sorry, MonicaRubbing her bloodshot eyes, she quickly picked up her glasses and put them back on. \lquote Why haven\rquote t you gone home yet, dear?\rdblquote\par
Seeing her teacher smiling again, Monica was relieved. Her princess teacher was back. \lquote Because you looked like you were sick. Don\rquote t die, Miss Llewellyn,\rdblquote  she said, bursting into tears.\par
Seeing Monica in this state snapped Layla out of her daze.\par
\ldblquote That\rquote s what happened to my mommy,\rdblquote  Monica said. \ldblquote She looked sick like you, and then she went up to heaven.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t worry, Monica. I\rquote m not sick. I\rquote m fine. I promise. Okay?\ldblquote  Layla said, quickly rising from her seat and embracing the crying girl.\par
I couldn\rquote t even hide my feelings from a child, she thought. How pathetic.\par
Once Monica had finished crying, she said \ldblquote Here, I made this for you.\rdblquote  She held out a bouquet of wildflowers she had gathered. They were already wilted, but the little yellow flowers warmed Layla\rquote s heart.\par
\ldblquote Thank you, Monica.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Don\rquote t get sick, okay?\rquote  \ldblquote Okay, I won\rquote t.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You promise?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I promise,\rdblquote  Layla replied, nodding her head.\par
Monica smiled. Layla took out her handkerchief and wiped away Monica\rquote s tears, then tidied her messy hair.\par
Monica then left, leaving the classroom in silence, the way it usually was in the late afternoon. Layla sat at her desk for a while longer, then grabbed her bag and stood up.\par
\par
That morning, after several days of silence, Uncle Bill had finally spoken, saying, \rquote Let\rquote s go, Layla.\rdblquote\par
She had been about to leave for work when he had walked up to her with a determined look on his face and continued, saying, I won\rquote t let him have you for another day. Let\rquote s go somewhere far away, where the duke can never find you.\rdblquote  As he said this, his eyes had been filled with cold rage.\par
Layla had found his proposal daunting, but she had immediately nodded. She had already been planning to leave, anyway. She had never intended for Uncle Bill to have to suffer the misfortune of finding out about everything, but there was no way to undo that now that it had happened.\par
Now she only had one remaining wish, and that was to leave the duke. She could go anywhere in the world, as long as he wasn\rquote t there. She wanted to completely remove him from her life, so that she and Uncle Bill could live an ordinary life together again.\par
Rubbing her bloodshot eyes, she quickly climbed onto her bicycle. As she left the city and turned onto the road leading to Arvis, she remembered the afternoon when she had ridden in the mail coach to meet Uncle Bill.\par
With her arms wrapped around her worn-out suitcase, she had practiced her smile and earnestly prayed that Uncle Bill would like her. She had promised herself that if he did, she would be a good girl.\par
She had tried so hard to keep that promise, but in the end she had broken it. She had committed an unforgivable wrong, and broken Uncle Bill\rquote s heart.\par
I never should\rquote ve come here, she thought. Instead of crossing the border from Lovita to Berg, I should\rquote ve gone to an orphanage. Then I never would\rquote ve met the duke, and I wouldn\rquote t have had to endure ah this pain. More importantly, Kyle and Uncle Bill wouldn\rquote t have had their hearts broken.\par
\par
However, she realized with self-disgust that even if she were able to do it all over again, she still would have held out hope and gone to see Uncle Bill.\par
Just then, a car turned around the bend in the road. It was the duke\rquote s car.\par
