The embarrassment in the young woman\rquote s eyes disappeared, leaving in its place a relaxed warmth similar to the warmth of the river at the height of summer. As if to dive into that warm river, he extended his hand to her. She hesitated for a moment, and then accepted his hand. As they stood facing each other, a refreshing breeze blew past them. He chuckled, and she smiled back at him. It was a beautiful smile.\par
If our relationship had started like that, would things have ended up differently between us? he wondered.\par
To rid his mind of these meaningless thoughts, he started walking at a faster pace down the forest path. He could feel his throat twitching and his fists quivering with his quickening breath.\par
As he stepped out of the forest and came to a stop in front of the gardener\rquote s cottage, he once again said, \ldblquote Layla\'85\rdblquote\par
In the same way that his canary flew to him when he whistled for it, Layla came running to him and held his hand. They spent the day together on a carefree stroll through the forest and along the riverside.\par
She giggled, played with him, and sometimes flirted with him. As she chatted with him about this and that, her singsong voice was clear and beautiful. Even though he hadn\rquote t clipped her wings or locked the door of her cage, his beautiful bird didn\rquote t try to escape.\par
If our days together had been like this, would you still be here with me? he wondered.\par
Another breeze blew in from the river, shaking the leaves of the forest trees again. As he walked along the margin between the forest and the river, he\par
\par
looked up at the bright, dazzling sky. It was too bright for him, so he closed his eyes. Even then it felt too bright, so he put his hand over his face. However, he didn\rquote t stop walking. He felt like he was being carried on by momentum, just like the wheels of the overturned bicycle.\par
Summer arrived, and he got married, just as planned. He raised the veil, and the dreamily beautiful, green eyes of his bride gazed shyly back at him. Her cheeks were pink like fresh roses.\par
He called her name: \ldblquote Layla\'85\rdblquote\par
She smiled. Each of them could tell what the other was thinking simply by their smiles and the looks in their eyes.\par
He gradually slowed his walking pace and came to a stop. His hands were still covering his face. Riding on those ever-spinning bicycle wheels, he traveled even further into his imagination.\par
It was summer again. He and Layla were walking along the riverside after a delicious meal together. Now it was another summer. Acute, blonde-haired toddler was taking some halting steps in the shade of a large tree. When he made eye contact with her, she shouted, \ldblquote Daddy!\rdblquote  and smiled excitedly. He smiled back at her and spread his arms out. She came running towards him and buried herself in his embrace. Layla smiled as she watched the father and daughter. Her smile looked just like the toddler\rquote s.\par
He wished that he could have had a life like that with Layla, if only it were possible.\par
After a long while, he lowered his hands from his face. His tears ran down the lines in his palms and dripped to the ground, leaving small, dark dots in the dry dirt.\par
He knew that ultimately none of what he had imagined was possible.\par
\par
The sunlight passed between the tree branches and shone on his wet, red eyes. Silently laughing, he closed his eyes again.\par
Just as the wheels of the overturned bicycle had eventually stopped spinning, he couldn\rquote t keep imagining things like this forever. Soon even the breeze stopped, leaving him alone in silence by the river.\par
\ldblquote When you think about everything you\rquote ve done to me, do you regret rt? Even a little bit ?\rdblquote\par
He felt like he could hear her faint voice asking him this question again.\par
He couldn\rquote t give a different answer than he had given that day. \ldblquote No,\rdblquote  he whispered. \ldblquote No, Layla.\rdblquote\par
He knew that, even if he were able to go back in time and do it all over again, nothing would have changed. As long as he was Duke Herhardt and she was Layla Llewellyn, their relationship would have started in the same way, as would his yearning for her. Even if he had known that she would have ended up running away from him, he still would have made exactly the same choices. Thus, he didn\rquote t regret anything having to do with her.\par
His hands stopped shaking. He buttoned the top button of his shirt again, tightened his tie, and adjusted the lapels of his jacket. The only remaining sign of his inner turmoil was the moisture in his eyes and eyelashes, which the sunlight soon dried. Soon his face was back to its usual, calm but tired expression.\par
He arrived back at the mansion just as the sun was starting to set. Hessen, who had been standing anxiously in the main hall, ran up to him, saying, \ldblquote Your Grace!\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Sorry I took so long,\rdblquote  Matthias calmly replied. \ldblquote Has Count Klein arrived?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Yes, he\rquote s waiting for you in the third-floor office.\rdblquote\par
\par
The duke strode up the stairs towards his study. He was more than thirty minutes late for the meeting. It was quite rude, but considering that Count Klein had once shown up an hour late for a meeting, he knew it wouldn\rquote t be the end of the world.\par
He soon arrived at the door of the small study that he used as an office.\par
After putting on his Duke Herhardt face, he opened the door.\par
Nothing had changed. It had to be that way.\par
