The sunlight shimmered on her bangs as they fluttered in the salty sea breeze.\par
\ldblquote But Layla, are you really going to start tomorrow? Isn\rquote t that a little soon? I think you should rest a while longer and get better first.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Do you really think anyone looking at me would say I look ill?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Well you haven\rquote t been eating well. If that\rquote s not an illness, what is it?\rdblquote\par
\par
Despite his blunt way of speaking, his eyes were filled with deep concern.\par
Ever since the move, Layla hadn\rquote t been eating well. Often she had no appetite at all. When she did manage to choke down even a small amount of food, she often threw it up afterwards. It was causing Bill a great deal of anxiety.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m fine, Uncle Bill. I think working will be much better for me than staying at home. It\rquote ll help me adjust to life here faster, too.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I hope you\rquote re right about that\'85\rdblquote\par
He sighed as he looked at her dramatically emaciated features. As much as he hated to see her in tears, if she were sobbing and moaning he could at least try to comfort her. The way she kept a smile plastered on her face while keeping her troubles bottled up inside made him feel even worse for her.\par
\ldblquote U-uncle Bill! This is our place right here,\rdblquote  she shouted, grabbing Bill\rquote s arm as he started to walk past their building.\par
He glanced briefly over his shoulder, then casually kept walking. \ldblquote I know.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Then where are you going?\rdblquote\par
