The first crack in Matthias\rquote s orderly life emerged during the night. He wasn\rquote t sleeping well. As the days went by, his sleepless hours each night had Increased, until he was barely sleeping at all. The deep fatigue was making his every movement feel laborious and difficult, as if his body were made of wet rags. He could no longer deny the fact that his mind was so foggy as to be almost incapable of sustaining focus on anything.\par
He got out of bed later than normal. He had already been dead tired the previous evening. After another sleepless night, his face was visibly gaunt. The weight loss had made his facial features sharper and more defined, giving him an even colder appearance than usual.\par
He went into the bathroom and stared at his bloodshot eyes in the mirror. He looked ridiculously haggard, but he didn\rquote t snicker at himself like he had used to do. At some point this habit of his had stopped. He tried to remember how long this had been the case, but it was hard to recall. Rationally he knew it couldn\rquote t have been more than a few days, and yet it felt like the distant past.\par
\par
He slowly ran his hands over his face, then turned away from the mirror.\par
During his shower, his headache became even more severe.\par
After the shower, he put on a robe and stepped out of the bathroom, and soon heard a knock at the door.\par
\ldblquote Your Grace, it\rquote s me, Hessen.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Come in,\rdblquote  he replied, sitting down on a chair next to the window.\par
He unconsciously shifted his gaze out the window, and saw the garden down below spotted with countless, brightly colored rosebuds. The spring sunlight shone radiantly on the road, as if to draw attention to the lack of a young woman hurrying off to work.\par
It was an ordinary morning. He read in the newspaper about all the tumultuous goings-on in the world, and he listened to Hessen telling him the day\rquote s schedule. He sipped hot coffee as he squinted in the bright sunlight. Then he heard another disappointing report on the status of the search.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m sorry. Your Grace. We have still been unable to find any clues as to the gardener and Layla Llewellyn\rquote s whereabouts,\rdblquote  Hessen sorrowfully reported.\par
Matthias gave the same response he had given the day before, and the day before that: \ldblquote Find them.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes, Your Grace. I\rquote ll give it my best,\rdblquote  Hessen replied, trying not to let the helplessness he was feeling show on his face.\par
Matthias nodded in response. His extreme fatigue was clearly visible.\par
Hessen had long since noticed that the duke\rquote s daily life was growing increasingly disordered. He had stayed silent about this up to now, knowing that the duke would prefer it that way, but he no longer felt that he could stand by and watch his master waste away.\par
Having made up his mind, he said, \ldblquote Your Grace, I\rquote m going to have Dr.\par
Ettman come here after he gets home from work\rdblquote\par
\par
Matthias narrowed his eyes questionings at him.\par
However, Hessen didn\rquote t back down. \ldblquote You need to see a doctor. Your Grace.\par
Please, just take it as a personal request from your aged butler.\rdblquote\par
No matter how young and virile the duke was, no one could last forever without sleeping or eating properly. Perhaps sensing Hessen\rquote s concern, the duke yielded to his suggestion.\par
\ldblquote Fine,\rdblquote  he softly replied, setting down the coffee cup he had been holding without taking a sip. \ldblquote Go ahead and call him.\rdblquote\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
The calm expression on Dr. Ettman\rquote s face as he entered the master suite soon gave way to a look of deep concern.\par
The duke was sitting on a chair in his bedroom, waiting for him. The duke\rquote s facial expression, bearing, and gestures as he greeted the doctor looked like the usual Duke Herhardt, but his face was noticeably gaunt. However, he still had his usual penetrating gaze, which gave him a strange air.\par
After the doctor concluded a brief examination, Matthias said, \ldblquote Some sleeping pills should be enough.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote But Your Grace, I think I should conduct a more thorough examination before-\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote There\rquote s nothing wrong with me other than a lack of sleep.\rdblquote  \ldblquote You seemed to have lost a lot of weight, too.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Once I get some rest, my appetite is sure to return as well\rdblquote  The duke\rquote s tone was flat and unemotional, but his intention was clear.\par
\par
Dr. Ettman was concerned, but he decided not to press the matter any further on the stubborn duke. It was clear at a glance that the duke wasn\rquote t sleeping well. Dr. Ettman would have preferred to pursue other forms of treatment before relying on medication, but the most important thing for the duke right now was that he get some proper sleep, and in his current condition sleeping pills seemed like the only way.\par
What could be going on with him? Dr. Ettman wondered. He wasn\rquote t aware of anything in the duke\rquote s life that could be causing this sleeplessness. However, he decided not to dwell on the question, knowing that as a doctor his curiosity was not supposed to extend beyond what was strictly necessary for the care of his patient. Instead, he politely left. Hessen, who had been waiting quietly in the corner, followed him out.\par
Left alone in his room, Matthias flung his tired body onto the bed. He felt like he was submerged in a bottomless pool. He felt so tired that it seemed like he could fall into a deep sleep if he just closed his eyes. However, whenever he did close his eyes, his painfully tense mind only seemed to grow more restless.\par
By the time the courier arrived at the mansion with the bottle of sleeping pills Dr. Ettman had sent, the sun was already down.\par
Matthias, who had been lying awake on his bed, opened his eyes at the sound of Hessen knocking. It was dark in the unlit bedroom.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote ll have your dinner sent to your bedroom,\rdblquote  Hessen said, placing the pill bottle on the bedside table.\par
Matthias shifted his gaze from the ceiling to the butler. \ldblquote No. There\rquote s no need for that.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote But, Your Grace\'85\rdblquote  Hessen trailed off, noticing that Matthias had closed his eyes. \ldblquote Sleep well then. Your Grace.\rdblquote\par
\par
Matthias then heard the sound of the butler quietly leaving the room. He kept his eyes closed for a long while after that.\par
Where was the line between sincerity and deceit with her?\par
He had become fixated on this question ever since he had realized that she had deceived him. He was sure it hadn\rquote t all been a lie. It couldn\rquote t have been. Thus he felt he had to figure out what aspects of her behavior and bearing had been sincere. So far, however, he didn\rquote t have a clue.\par
Her lovely face, her bashful smile, and her warm touch. At times it all had seemed sincere. At other times it had all seemed like a ruse.\par
He desperately racked his brain looking for an answer, believing that if he could simply determine a clear line between her sincere and deceitful behavior, the rest of his life would fall back into place. However, no matter how much he tried to figure it out, he always came up short. Giving up for the moment, he opened his eyes and sat up, leaning against the headboard of his bed.\par
Staring into the darkness, he tried whistling. However, his bird did not sing in response. The door of its cage was open, but it just remained hunched up in its nest. He tried whistling for it again and again, but there was still no response.\par
He turned his blank gaze to the bedside table, where the bottle of sleeping pills was sitting. His preference not to rely on such things was in direct conflict with his desperate yearning for sleep.\par
After a long deliberation, he swallowed one of the pills and flopped back, down onto the bed. As soon as he did, a dry laugh escaped his lips. As the medicine gradually took effect, a feeling of relief spread through his body.\par
Everything will be aft right now, he thought to himself. If I can just get to sleep, and then wake up again, then everything will be all right.\par
