Matthias slowly opened his eyes, then remained lying for a long while, staring into space. The curtains were drawn, so it was dark in his room. Considering that he had fallen asleep just as the sun was starting to rise, however, he was fairly sure that morning had already passed.\par
Ever since he had started taking sleeping pills, his sense of time had gotten fuzzy. Anytime he couldn\rquote t get to sleep, he would pop a pill, whether it was the early evening, or late at night. Sometimes he took them in the middle of the day.\par
\par
Sleep had become a place of refuge for him. While he was immersed in medicated slumber, he wasn\rquote t tormented by memories of Layla. Of course, however, these memories came flooding back as soon as he awoke.\par
He no longer liked doing anything but sleeping. The family doctor had irritatingly started paying him regular visits, even though he didn\rquote t recall requesting this.\par
On his visit the previous day, Dr Ettman had warned that he would be forced to discontinue his prescription for the sleeping pills if the duke kept abusing them. Matthias had simply chuckled briefly in response. He knew the doctor wasn\rquote t the type to give such a warning lightly, and might very well cut off his prescription. But he also knew that as Duke Herhardt, he could obtain anything he wanted. If Dr. Ettman stopped prescribing the sleeping pills, he could just get another doctor to prescribe them for him.\par
Moving very slowly, he got out of bed. After drinking a glass of water and taking a shower, he looked at the clock and saw that it was already past noon. He tried to recall his schedule for the day, but nothing came to mind, so he rang the service bell to summon the butler and started to get dressed.\par
Just as he was buttoning the last button of his shirt, Hessen arrived. \ldblquote I\rquote ll have your lunch brought up, Your Grace.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No need. Coffee will be enough for me.\rdblquote  \ldblquote But Your Grace, I\'85\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote What does my afternoon look like?\rdblquote  As Matthias asked this, cutting the butler off, he walked over to the window and opened the curtains. There were dark clouds covering the sky. The whole world looked gray. He opened the window, and a humid breeze blew in, full of the sweet scent of flowers.\par
\ldblquote You don\rquote t have anything scheduled for the afternoon,\rdblquote  Hessen replied. \ldblquote You should just get some rest.\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote I see.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You have to eat, Your Grace. Or would you like me to call Dr. Ettman?\rdblquote  Hessen was being more persistent than usual.\par
Brushing back his still-wet hair, Matthias turned to face him. The aged butler normally showed no emotion, but now he was staring at the duke with eyes full of worry.\par
Do I look that bad?\par
He was too tired to dwell on the question. He felt like going back to sleep, and only waking up again once all these distractions had vanished. That was all he wanted. He didn\rquote t want food, he didn\rquote t want to see the doctor, he just wanted to sleep.\par
After taking a sip of the coffee that Hessen had brought for him, he walked over to the unusually quiet birdcage. His canary, which had used to excitedly hop and fly all around the room, had lately been staying hunched up in the nest within its cage.\par
He reached into the cage and carefully retrieved the limp, quivering bird. Viewed up close, it was clearly sick. Its feathers had lost their beautiful luster, and now looked rough and unkempt.\par
It pained him to look at the state the bird was in and remember how it had used to bathe and preen itself several times a day. Just then, the canary started rubbing its beak and body against his hand, as if it was trying to absorb his body heat.\par
Wrapping both his hands around it to keep it warm, he sat staring blankly for a long time, while memories of that day cycled through his mind.\par
\ldblquote I want you to love me.\rdblquote\par
She had smiled at him like a witch putting a curse on him that day. \ldblquote I would like it so much if you loved me "\par
\par
Those sweet words were the dagger she had driven into his heart that late afternoon.\par
\ldblquote I want you to love me deeply. Forever and ever.\rdblquote  My ever so beautiful, ever so cruel Layla\'85\par
Carefully placing the bird back in its nest, he said, \ldblquote Have the bird-keeper take a look at this.\rdblquote  His voice was hoarse with agitation.\par
\ldblquote Pardon? Ah\'85 Yes, Your Grace, I will,\rdblquote  Hessen replied.\par
Matthias then turned and went out the door. Hessen hurried behind him, saying, \ldblquote If you\rquote re going on an outing, I should let Evers know-\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m just going for a walk. I don\rquote t need my assistant.\rdblquote  Matthias came to a stop again at the window in the hallway.\par
Hessen hesitated for a moment, then took his leave and walked away. Only then did Matthias continue striding down the hallway. By the time he had exited the mansion and entered the garden, dark rain clouds were covering the sky, but he didn\rquote t care.\par
He walked and walked. He didn\rquote t know where he was headed, but it didn\rquote t matter anyway.\par
After the days when he had snickered at Layla\rquote s cute behavior had passed, he had been flooded with wild feelings of anger. Looking back on it, he was shocked that he had been deceived by such a terrible actress. He had been blinded by lust. His anger and self-loathing grew until they felt like they were going to swallow him up. But now, even they seemed to have vanished. He would have preferred to feel some anger, rather than the frigid, still feeling in his chest.\par
Is it all over, then?\par
As he asked himself this, he arrived at the river. Just like the dark sky, the water was murky. Not wanting to stop, he walked along the riverside,\par
\par
following its current.\par
If it\rquote s all over, then there\rquote s no reason to find her, is there? he asked himself.\par
Perhaps it was for the best. His beautiful misfortune had vanished. This had caused him some mild distress, but now that distress had vanished as well, and he could finally return to his stable life as Duke Herhardt.\par
Yes, it would be easy, like everything in his life ultimately was.\par
As he was snickering at this thought, he saw a bird fly up from the riverbank ahead of him. Glancing indifferently at it at first, he quickly noticed that it had a red string tied to one of its legs. It was a migratory bird that Layla had been tracking.\par
It came back, he thought. He could feel his heart beating faster. The bird you were waiting for came back, Layla. Soon his heart was pounding so hard, he felt like it was making his whole body shake. So you have to come back too, Layla.\par
Suddenly he felt like Layla really might return, or perhaps that she was already back. A twisted grin appeared on his face. He knew that he was passing into delusion, but he couldn\rquote t stop. The next thing he knew, he was running down the forest path.\par
As a gentle spring rain started to fall, he emerged from the forest and saw the gardener\rquote s cottage.\par
White sheets fluttered on the clothesline. The cottage\rquote s windows glowed warmly from within. Wearing an apron, Layla came running out through the open front door. As she hurriedly gathered the laundry to save it from the rain, her French braid bobbed up and down.\par
Look at that. She came back.\par
He started laughing wildly. So this is what I\rquote ve become, he thought, realizing that his mental disturbance over the past few weeks had been more serious than he had thought. Shortly after his breathless laughter burst forth, however,\par
