They walked down the street, holding hands. At first Bill tried to pull his hand away, but Layla was persistent. Eventually he gave in and gladly held her hand.\par
As they happened to pass their downstairs neighbor, she smiled and walked up to them, saying, \ldblquote Miss Llewellyn! Are you out on a walk with your father?\rdblquote\par
Everyone in the neighborhood assumed that Bill and Layla were father and daughter. Layla had no desire to correct this misapprehension, so she nodded. \ldblquote Yes, I am.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote What did she say?\rdblquote  Bill asked once the neighbor was out of earshot.\par
\ldblquote She asked if I was out on a walk with my father. So I said yes,\rdblquote  she replied, looking sheepishly up at him.\par
\par
Father\'85 ? Bill mulled over this word for a moment, then laughed it off. That poor girl gave her body to the duke in order to buy my freedom. I don\rquote t deserve to be called her father. Still, I like the ring of it\'85 Father\'85 While he felt a little guilty, hearing her call him her father filled him with pride.\par
Is she going to keep calling me that\'85 ? he wondered as they continued to walk to the square. He continued to think about it as they ate lunch and went on a walk along the seaside. Just as he was about to ask her if she intended to keep calling her his father, he looked over at her smiling face and lost the nerve to say anything. He had a tingling feeling in his chest. It felt somewhat like he was being tickled with a feather, and somewhat like he was being stabbed with a knife. Either way, he felt very guilty.\par
Just as he was starting to feel tears welling up in his eyes, they arrived at the beach. Clean, white sand extended for miles up the coast. There seemed to be some sort of lively summer festival going on that weekend. The vendors\rquote  colorful stalls and parasols made it look like a flower garden.\par
They bought ice creams and ale them together. Lately Layla had been so hesitant to eat anything, Bill had worried that she might be sick. She didn\rquote t hesitate at all in devouring the ice cream, however.\par
They started walking down the beach. Layla looked very happy and excited as she walked with her shoes and socks off. They laughed merrily. Neither of them could remember when they had last had such a good time. They decided to focus on the present. That was all that really mattered, after all.\par
As the sun was setting, they returned home and finished off their day. The following day wasn\rquote t much different.\par
What does it matter what she calls me? Bill thought, looking at Layla across the breakfast table on Monday morning. No matter what she calls me, the fact remains that we\rquote re family.\par
\par
Soon, he noticed that she kept glancing at him furtively. \ldblquote Layla, is there something you want to talk to me about?\rdblquote  he asked.\par
Now that he thought about it, she had been glancing at him now and then since the previous day.\par
She paused for a moment, then replied, \ldblquote No\'85 I don\rquote t. We should get going.\par
It\rquote s time to leave for work.\rdblquote\par
She was smiling brightly, but he still felt suspicious.\par
Something was clearly on her mind. What could she be hiding? He tried to think what it could be, but nothing came to mind. Meanwhile, they reached the square, which was the spot where they always parted ways to go to their respective workplaces.\par
Normally the square was full of bustling energy at this time of morning, but today for some reason it was quiet and solemn. A large crowd of people was gathered around the bulletin board in front of City Hall. They seemed sad and morose, as if they were at a funeral.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m going to go see what\rquote s going on,\rdblquote  Layla told Bill.\par
As she entered the crowd, she heard mournful sighs and sobs all around her. She discovered the reason for this as soon as she got to the front of the crowd and read the notice on the bulletin board.\par
A wartime mobilization order had been declared, ordering all men eligible for conscription to enlist in the military.\par
