The retching sounds continued for a while before finally subsiding. Kyle stood up unsteadily, then staggered a few steps to sit and catch his breath in the shade of a nearby tree. As the army surgeons busily went in and out of the field hospital, a strong scent of blood and antiseptic wafted off of them.\par
Kyle felt another wave of nausea coming on, but luckily it died down on its own. It seemed that his body had given up, now that it had nothing left to expel.\par
A surgeon approached him and asked, \ldblquote You feeling any better?\rdblquote  The question snapped Kyle out of his daze. \ldblquote Not really. Have a seat\rdblquote\par
The surgeon sat down next to Kyle and offered him his canteen. The surgeon had graduated from the same medical school where Kyle was studying. Of all the men in the unit, he was the only one who looked after Kyle.\par
\par
Accepting the canteen from him, Kyle took a swig of water, swished it around in his mouth, and then spit it out. Then he took a large gulp of water and swallowed it. As his mind became clearer, the screams coming from inside the barracks grew clearer as well. It sounded like injured soldiers were waking up after surgery.\par
\ldblquote How much longer until we get to Sienne?\rdblquote  Kyle asked in an exhausted voice as he gazed up at the treetops. By this time of year, the trees back in Karlsbar were already starting to change color, but here in Lovita they were still green.\par
\ldblquote At our current rate, we should be arriving there very soon,\rdblquote  the surgeon replied, patting Kyle\rquote s shoulder to comfort him. \ldblquote I know it\rquote s tough, but try to hang in there, Kyle. Luckily, our side is doing much better in the war. If our luck holds up. we might be home by winter\rdblquote\par
Kyle had been hearing optimistic promises like this every day, while the bloodshed and gore only grew all the time. Nonetheless, he nodded. If the gruesome injuries he was seeing in the field hospital were what winning looked like, he didn\rquote t want to imagine what things looked like for the losing side.\par
The war had turned his life upside overnight. Shortly after the shocking mobilization announcement, he had been conscripted, as had many other medical students from his program.\par
The impression that the war had been unexpected was quickly dispelled. The empire was carrying out a detailed war plan, which they had already had in place prior to the declaration of war. The task of drafting millions of troops, training and arming them, and then sending them to the front lines was accomplished in the blink of an eye. It was frightening how quick and efficient the process was. Once Kyle arrived at the front line, he realized that behind the\par
\par
scenes, the empire had been planning for war for a long time, while waiting for just the right time and pretext.\par
He was in a complete daze. By the time he came to his senses, he was already at the battlefield. He had been assigned to be a medic for a unit that was attacking the southern line. He had barely completed one year of college, but since he was a student at the empire\rquote s best medical school, he was occasionally summoned to the field hospital to help out in the operation room. This tended to happen when a lot of wounded soldiers had just come in, as was the case today.\par
