Even once it was clear that the bombing had stopped, no one was eager to go outside.\par
As if the previous air raid had been just a warning, this time Beloff\rquote s planes had showered the city with much more ferocity. From the basement, there was no way to know what things looked like up at ground level, but it was clear that the damage would far exceed a single fallen bell tower like last time.\par
A baby started to cry, breaking the tense silence in the dark, stuffy basement. A number of small children then started crying as well, soon followed by the sound of adults sighing in exasperation.\par
Layla rubbed her cold hands together and tried to catch her breath. Her stomach, which felt like it was tied up in a knot, gradually started to relax as well. Just as she was regaining her composure, she became suddenly aware of an intense pain in her knee.\par
\par
She couldn\rquote t remember clearly how she had gotten to where she was now. After barely managing to make it out of the apartment building, she had found the street packed with people panicking over the attack. Someone had then bumped into her and she had fallen to the ground, losing her glasses. Just as she had found them and put them on again, another deafening explosion had rung out from a nearby street.\par
Ignoring the blood flowing from her scraped knee, she had started running, not stopping until she had safely reached the church basement. It wasn\rquote t clear to her how much time had passed since then, or what had happened in that time.\par
\ldblquote We can go back up now! The planes are gone!\rdblquote  shouted a young man who had gone upstairs to see the state of things at ground level.\par
Even after hearing this, however, no one moved. After a short feeling of relief, the basement was filled with even louder cries and sighs.\par
Layla was the first to get up. She walked over towards the staircase, where a dim lamp was burning.\par
I have to find Uncle Bill.\par
This was the first thought that came to her mind when she regained her senses.\par
\ldblquote Are you going to be all right?\rdblquote  a man asked her, noticing that she was alone.\par
She nodded and hurried up to the street. The smell of dust and explosives permeated the clear night air. Smoke was drifting from the remains of ruined buildings all around. It appeared that the seaside village and harbor had been hit even more heavily than the city.\par
She took off her glasses with quivering hands. After rubbing her eyes vigorously, she put her glasses back on, but the scene before her had not\par
\par
changed at all. One of the lenses of her glasses was cracked, but she didn\rquote t mind. In this moment, she preferred not seeing things with too much clarity.\par
Uncle Bill probably took shelter somewhere around the harbor. Should I go look for him there? Or would it be better to go back to the apartment and wait for him there?\par
After dithering for a moment, she started walking back to the apartment.\par
She was missing a shoe, but she didn\rquote t care.\par
If I can just find Uncle Bill, everything will be all right. So I just have to get to- \ldblquote Layla!\rdblquote\par
Just as she was drawing close to the apartment building, she heard a familiar voice. Turning to look, she saw Allen, the man who had helped her and Uncle Bill find their apartment. He worked at the harbor with Uncle Bill, but for some reason he was alone.\par
\ldblquote Where\rquote s Uncle Bill? Did he go up into the apartment already?\rdblquote  she asked, limping towards him. \ldblquote You and he must have evacuated together, right? I was worried, but I\rquote m so glad to see you. Uncle Bill is up in the apartment, right? He must be so worried about me. I\rquote d better go see him.\rdblquote  She was speaking faster than normal.\par
Allen finally broke his silence, saying, \ldblquote Layla, don\rquote t be too shocked when I tell you this.\rdblquote  His voice cracked as soon as he started speaking.\par
The world seemed to start spinning, but Layla kept her eyes fixed on Allen. \ldblquote Bill is\'85 well, he\rquote s in the hospital.\rdblquote  The tears he had been holding back\par
started to fall. \ldblquote You\rquote ve got to go to him, Layla. Right now!\par
