Hearing a vague buzzing in his ears, Bill opened his eyes. For some reason, his vision was blurry, and he felt like he was submerged in deep water. Eventually he would feel horrible pain throughout his whole body, as if all of his bones were broken. At this point, though, he could hardly feel a thing.\par
\ldblquote Uncle Bill! Uncle Bill!\rdblquote\par
The hazy buzzing noise in his ears was pierced by the sound of Layla breathlessly sobbing and calling his name. He turned his head and looked in the direction the sound was coming from. Strangely, he wasn\rquote t able to move any other part of his body, but he could manage to turn his head.\par
Layla\'85 Why is she crying like that? He wanted to console her, but he couldn\rquote t get his voice to come out properly. Seeing people in white clothes busily walking to and fro, he could tell that he was in a hospital. Oh, yeah, maybe I did get hurt a little.\par
His memories abruptly cut off after the explosion at the harbor warehouse.\par
He had been carrying a box of mortar shells to a warship. There had been an evacuation order, and all the workers had started running frantically. Going with the flow of the crowd, he had run straight across the warehouse. Just as he thought he had made it outside, a deafening noise had rung out, as if the sky were falling. It had reminded him of the noise the mansion\rquote s generator had made when it exploded.\par
If only I hadn\rquote t broken that damn thing, I wouldn\rquote t have made Layla do the things she did, he had suddenly thought with regret. He had then felt a great\par
\par
deal of pain, before losing consciousness. He had no other memories after that point, other than the vague sensation of being buried underneath something.\par
Don\rquote t cry, Layla.\par
Layla was now struggling and wailing. He could see that she was holding his hand, but he couldn\rquote t feel a thing.\par
I\rquote d better get up, he decided. However, no matter how he tried, his body wouldn\rquote t cooperate with his will.\par
Meanwhile, the doctor ran over and pulled Layla away from him, then said something to her, after which she froze.\par
Stupid doctor, what sort of nonsense is he scaring her with?\par
The image of Layla\rquote s frightened, no longer crying face painfully imprinted itself into Bill\rquote s wavering consciousness.\par
Just because he\rquote s a doctor doesn\rquote t mean he knows anything. It\rquote s all right, Layla.\par
This is nothing.\par
He opened his lips to laugh, but all that came out was a faint moan and the sound of his labored breathing.\par
