\par
What is he saying? Layla shook her head, unable to understand what the doctor was getting at.\par
After being taken to the hospital, she had found Uncle Bill lying in a bed, covered with blood and bruises. A doctor had told her that he had been severely injured when the waterfront warehouse he was working in collapsed during the air raid.\par
She couldn\rquote t believe what she was seeing. Nor could she accept what the doctor was telling her.\par
\ldblquote N-no. Y-you must be mistaken,\rdblquote  she stammered. \ldblquote I think he was waiting for his daughter to arrive.\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t say that. Doctor, you need to operate on him right away, please\'85\rdblquote  \ldblquote You should say your final farewell to him before it\rquote s too late,\rdblquote  the doctor\par
said, avoiding Layla\rquote s gaze.\par
Layla\rquote s legs started to give out. A nurse rushed over to help her stay standing.\par
Brushing the nurse away, Layla limped over to Bill\rquote s bedside and started wailing like an injured animal.\par
\ldblquote Uncle Bill! You can hear me, right? G-get up, right now! Let\rquote s go home, okay?\rdblquote\par
As she cried and clung to him, Bill managed to open his eyes. Sobs and moans flowed from his lips as he weakly twisted his injured body, trying to get up somehow. Eventually the doctor couldn\rquote t stand by and watch anymore. He rushed towards them and pulled Layla from his side.\par
\ldblquote If you keep saying things like that, you\rquote ll make his final moments even worse. I know it\rquote s hard, but the best thing to do right now is try to help him pass on in comfort.\rdblquote\par
His final moments\'85?\par
\par
When she heard that phrase, the tears stopped falling from her eyes, and she became aware of a truth that she desperately wanted to deny. Perhaps she had been vaguely aware of this truth the moment she had first entered the hospital room and seen him.\par
Gasping like a choking person, she staggered back to the bedside. Smiling and crying at once, she looked at Uncle Bill.\par
