\ldblquote You always said that you knew I would become an outstanding adult. You still think so, right? You still believe in me?\rdblquote\par
Does she really have to ask?\par
Bill almost burst into laughter at Layla\rquote s obvious question. Instead, however, all that came out as a series of burning coughs, after which some sort of hot liquid started seeping out of his mouth.\par
A nurse rushed over and put a towel to his mouth. Layla looked away for a moment. When she looked back, she was smiling even more brightly, while even thicker tears ran down her cheeks.\par
\ldblquote If you believe in me, I can do anything. Thanks to you, Uncle Bill, I am an outstanding adult now, and I\rquote m making a good life for myself.\rdblquote\par
As Bill slipped in and out of consciousness, he heard Layla\rquote s faint voice as she continued to speak to him. At the same time, short memories of days past kept flashing through his mind.\par
He recalled the spring day when he was planting rose seedlings, and a little girl had arrived riding in the mail coach. All the days he had spent with her\par
\par
seemed to be infused with the fresh scent of flowers and herbs. Every single day with her had been beautiful.\par
I can\rquote t let this happen. He twisted his body with all his might. I can\rquote t leave her alone in this cruel world. I can\rquote t\'85\par
\ldblquote Uncle Bill!\rdblquote\par
A few men in white clothes ran over and grabbed him. Layla put her little hand to his cheek. Tears flowed from his eyes, wetting her hand like rain on a flower petal.\par
\ldblquote I was so happy when you wanted to be my family, Uncle Bill. I was full of joy, every day. Truly. We were always a real family, and we will keep being one, right?\rdblquote\par
Bill\rquote s eyes filled with tears. Of course. Of course we will.\par
He wanted to respond to her, but her face was gradually growing hazy.\par
\ldblquote If we\rquote re born again in another life, I\rquote ll find you and become part of your family again. It will just be a brief parting, and then a reunion.\rdblquote  She pressed her lips against his bushy beard. Amid her sobs, she whispered, \ldblquote I love you, Dad.\rdblquote\par
He was barely hanging onto consciousness now, but he heard the last thing she whispered loud and clear. As he stopped his painful struggling, a sad smile spread across his bloodstained lips.\par
Suddenly he remembered a leisurely summer evening, perhaps the same year she had first come to him. They had walked through the woods and along the riverside. The breeze had shaken the little girl\rquote s hair and clothes as she walked alongside him, reciting the names of flowers and trees that he had taught her.\par
She had excitedly run a short distance ahead, picked up a shiny pebble and a white feather, and slowly walked back to his side. Then she had furtively\par
\par
placed her hand into his huge hand. Her tiny hand had felt very warm as it struggled to wrap around his, only making it around a couple of his fingers.\par
She had looked up at him with hesitation, but when he had chuckled, she had started smiling shyly and squeezed his hand more tightly. They had continued to walk along the riverside until the purple twilight had given way to darkness.\par
Yes, that\rquote s when I knew it.\par
All of a sudden, he realized something that he had known in the back of his mind for a long time, but had been too embarrassed to admit to himself.\par
Just then, as if God had changed His mind, Bill was able to utter a single sentence: \ldblquote I\'85 love you.\rdblquote\par
There we go. That wasn\rquote t so hard now, was it?\par
Along with a deep sense of relief, he could then feel the energy quickly draining from his body.\par
When I wake up, first thing I\rquote ll do is go buy a bunch of tasty food. We did say we were going to have a party. Her and me. I have so much to tell her, too. Maybe we\rquote ll stay up all night, drinking and chatting and having a good time. Well, maybe not drinking. She can\rquote t have alcohol anymore, so I\rquote d better get some apple juice or something instead. We can clink our glasses together, just like the day we first became a family.\par
Smiling, Bill whispered again, \ldblquote I love you, my daughter.\rdblquote\par
When I wake up, maybe I\rquote ll be embarrassed I said that, but oh well. It\rquote s not so bad. After all, it\rquote s true. And it will never stop being true.\par
