News about Sienne\rquote s fall spread quickly throughout the city. Posters announcing the imminent arrival of the occupying forces were posted\par
in various spots.\par
Layla hurried towards a street corner where a murmuring crowd of people was gathered in front of a notice board. The message on the poster was more or less what she had expected. It indicated that all weapons and communication equipment were to be rendered to the authorities, and that it was strictly forbidden for anyone to slander or attack the occupation forces. The overall message was that unconditional surrender and compliance were the only way to survive. It also indicated that no one was allowed to leave the city without a pass authorized by the general of the occupying forces.\par
The others in the crowd were clearly agitated, showing signs of fear, despair, and anger, but Layla was in a daze. She felt nothing particular about\par
\par
what she had just read. The war felt irrelevant, as did everything in her life.\par
After being pushed aside by other people eager to read the notice, she trudged back home. She had been in this state ever since Uncle Bill\rquote s funeral. Each day had passed by indifferently, as if she were dreaming. She felt like a dried-up plant in a discarded flowerpot.\par
She knew she couldn\rquote t go on like this, but she couldn\rquote t help it. It took all her willpower just to muster the strength to visit Uncle Bill\rquote s grave once a day.\par
She plodded up the steps and entered her apartment, then stood for a long while, feeling the breeze blowing in through the windows, which had been broken in the air raid.\par
The sky was clear and blue. There was yellow dust floating amid the crisp autumn sunlight. By all appearances it was a beautiful day, but she felt nothing but utter loneliness.\par
To resist acknowledging the fact that she was once again completely alone in life, she shouted, \ldblquote Uncle Bill!\rdblquote  After a moment, she forced a smile and shouted again even louder: \ldblquote Uncle Bill!!\rdblquote\par
She imagined hearing his heavy steps as he walked up the stairs and opened the door. For a moment, she could almost hear him laughing and greeting her: \ldblquote Hi, Layla!\rdblquote  However, the apartment was filled with nothing but oppressive silence.\par
Suddenly she missed the fallen bell tower. Its deafening ringing would have made things a little more bearable. In fact, any sound would have been preferable to this silence.\par
She felt like running out of the apartment but lacked the energy to do so. Instead, she went to her room and flopped down on the bed. Her bedroom window was the only one that hadn\rquote t completely broken in the air raid, but it had been cracked, and it was only a matter of time before it would break.\par
\par
I need to go buy some new window panes. But is that even possible in the current situation? What will I do when winter rolls around?\par
She lay motionless like a doll, staring into space as thought after thought passed through her mind.\par
Then it occurred to her that she was out of food as well. Her last meal had been some stale bread that she had choked down two days earlier. Suddenly she was hit with a wave of intense hunger, but she lacked the willpower to get up.\par
The threat of the occupying army\rquote s imminent arrival had thrown the city into disarray. On the way back from the cemetery she had passed by the grocery store. It had looked like a battlefield, with desperate citizens fighting over the little that was remained. She had no intention of entering into that fray, but she also knew that it would be irresponsible to simply starve. It was then that she remembered what Allen had given to her.\par
The weekend after Bill\rquote s funeral, Allen and his family had left Sienne. He had told Layla that they were headed to the capital, which was still safe, before the arrival of the Berg army would make travel impossible.\par
He had apologized to her repeatedly, telling her how sorry he was for leaving her alone in Sienne. However, Layla didn\rquote t blame him. She was just the adopted daughter of a distant relative of his who had shown up out of the blue one day. The kindness he had shown her already was enough for her see him as a good person. She didn\rquote t expect him to take her along with him when he was struggling to escape with his family.\par
Anyway, she had no desire to leave the city. No matter where she went, she could never be by Uncle Bill\rquote s side like she wanted. Seeing Allen unable to lift his head and face her made her feel even more guilty.\par
\par
Before leaving Sienne, he had stopped by the apartment to bid farewell to her, and had given her a large bundle of necessities \emdash food, fabric, and various odds and ends. She had felt very grateful for this and had thanked him profusely. He had stared back at her for a moment, rubbing his bloodshot eyes, then left. With that, yet another good person had departed from her life.\par
She took a jar of pickled apricots from the bundle. She carefully avoided looking at the small box full of Uncle Bill\rquote s belongings from the warehouse. She felt like if she were to look inside that box, things would really be over.\par
Sitting down at the kitchen table, she ravenously ate all the apricots in the jar, not even thinking to move them to a dish first. Wiping the sticky juice from her mouth with the back of her hand, she started laughing bitterly. She was still hungry and sleepy, and she hated herself for still wanting to live.\par
