The aide looked uneasily at the locked door.\par
Since abducting the woman and putting her in his room, whenever the duke left the room he would thoroughly lock it, using a chain and padlock. It seemed that the duke had truly become unhinged since entering Sienne.\par
The aide let out a deep sigh, then carefully unlocked the padlock. He didn\rquote t look forward to the sight that he knew was going to greet him upon entering the room, but he couldn\rquote t go against his superior\rquote s orders.\par
\par
After clearing his throat, he gently knocked on the door and said, \ldblquote I\rquote m coming in for a moment, Miss.\rdblquote  He then opened the door and stepped into the room.\par
The woman, who was sitting in front of the window that looked out onto the square, flinched and turned towards the door. The mess in the middle of the room had been cleaned up, but her face looked even paler than before.\par
\ldblquote What\rquote s that\'85?\rdblquote  she asked, narrowing her eyes at the covered tray the aide had placed on the table.\par
\ldblquote You should eat this,\rdblquote  he said. \ldblquote You have to eat to live, after all. And you should think of the baby. This has absolutely nothing to do with Major Herhardt.\rdblquote\par
The aide was doing his best to lie convincingly. If he were simply to let the woman go, everything would be resolved, but he knew he couldn\rquote t do that. He didn\rquote t understand why the major had gone to such trouble to drag her here and lock her up in the first place, but he knew it was his duty to carry out his orders.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote ve brought you this food without the major\rquote s knowledge. If he were to find out about this, I\rquote d be in a lot of trouble. He should be returning in about thirty minutes. You\rquote d better finish it before then,\rdblquote  he said, reciting the lines the major had given him verbatim.\par
The woman stared blankly at him for a moment, then looked at the tray again. It seemed that she didn\rquote t intend to beg him to let her go today. He was glad about this, as it had been very awkward having to turn her down previously.\par
Perhaps she had given up? It would make things much easier for him if she had, and yet it saddened him to think about it.\par
\par
After nervously fidgeting for a while, she said, \ldblquote So, could you tell me\'85 Is Kyle really in jail?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Kyle? You mean Kyle Ettman, the medic?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Yes, that\rquote s who I mean. Is he?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Ah\'85 yes, yes he is. He\rquote s serving a sentence for insubordination.\rdblquote  When he saw the woman\rquote s eyes fill with tears, he felt uncomfortable and looked away, before adding, \ldblquote It w-won\rquote t be a long sentence.\rdblquote\par
The woman nodded appreciatively at this last comment, though her face was still marked with deep sadness. \ldblquote Could you bring this food to Kyle instead, then?\rdblquote\par
His eyes widened at this unexpected request. \ldblquote Pardon?\rdblquote\par
It was hard to imagine something more foolhardy than to bring the food sent by the major not to its designated recipient, but to the man who was in jail for defying the major. If he were to do that, he was sure he would be immediately locked up right next to Kyle Ettman himself.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m afraid I won\rquote t be able to do that, Miss.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Oh\'85 okay,\rdblquote  she replied resignedly. \ldblquote I\rquote m sorry for asking. And thank you for bringing this.\rdblquote\par
The courtesy and submissiveness with which she was treating him made him feel even more guilty, so he hurried out the room. As he was closing the door, he was happy to see that the woman was lifting the cover from the tray. It was a sandwich and a glass of soda water. He had been surprised that an aristocratic officer like the major even knew of such cuisine, but he had faithfully followed the order. Hoping that the woman would eat it, he closed and locked the door again.\par
