My dream has come true!\par
As impossible as it seemed, Layla realized with joy that the dream she had held deep in her heart had become a reality.\par
It was the beginning of summer, when Arvis\rquote s forest was its most beautiful shade of green.\par
She was riding her bicycle excitedly towards the cottage. She was wearing the Gillis Girls\rquote  School summer uniform, a blue dress, and the hem of its skirt was flapping in the same breeze that was making the whole forest sway.\par
Her old bicycle creaked to a stop in front of the shed in the front yard of the cottage. She hopped off and scurried into the cottage. In no time, she had changed her clothes and come back outside again.\par
\par
Wearing her wide-brimmed straw hat and holding a huge basket, she walked with happy steps deep into the woods. Kyle would be coming over soon for a study session together. Before he arrived, she was going to pick as many raspberries as she could. She wanted to make sure she could make enough raspberry jam before summer was over, so that she would be able to afford to buy a pair of glasses.\par
It was the start of the summer of her eighteenth year, when her days were happy and full of joy.\par
She hummed to herself as she picked the raspberries. Now and then, she considered what to make for dinner. I\rquote ve got to make something that both Uncle Bill and Kyle will like. Maybe I should have asked Uncle Bill to catch a chicken this morning.\par
As she continued to diligently pick raspberries, she soon found herself by the river. A breeze full of the scent of the river tickled her sweat-beaded forehead.\par
Now every day is going to be like this, she thought with delight. Now that my dream has come true, I\rquote ll be able to live these days forever.\par
Just as her heart was filling with joy at this happy thought, she heard the sound of a child crying. That\rquote s strange. No kids live around here. As improbable as it seemed, the sound was too loud and clear for her to have misheard it.\par
She hesitated for a moment, then set down the heavy basket and walked towards where the sound was coming from. Before long, she was face-to-face with a young child she didn\rquote t recognize. The cute little child was about the same age she had been when her mother had abandoned her, and was walking along the riverside alone, sobbing.\par
Layla stepped back, feeling confused. I don\rquote t know who you are\'85\par
\par
She shook her head, trying to calm her pounding heart.\par
I\rquote ve got to get back to the cottage. Uncle Bill will be getting home soon. I have to make dinner for the three of us. But\'85 who\rquote s the kid? Why the tears?\par
Feeling like she was about to burst into tears herself, she took another step back. Meanwhile, the child\rquote s sobbing grew louder.\par
What do I do?\par
She bit her lip nervously. The child\rquote s crying grew louder and louder, until she couldn\rquote t hear anything else. It was then that she opened her eyes.\par
Her vision, blurry at first from the fever, gradually became clearer, until she saw a pair of blue eyes looking at her. Soon she recognized these eyes as belonging to Matthias, and then she realized that his large, strong hands were clutching her chin, forcing her mouth open. A moment later, she felt something cold and smooth touching her chapped lips. It was a medicine bottle.\par
