Matthias re-entered the room late that night. Kyle had already departed, so the room was empty other than Layla, who was fast asleep.\par
As her fever had dropped little by little, her breathing had grown smoother. Thus Matthias was able to gaze at her in deep silence for a long while. There was a calm expression on her face. Perhaps she was having a pleasant dream. He didn\rquote t know what her dream was about, but he did know one thing: he wasn\rquote t in it.\par
Not wanting to dwell on this unpleasant thought, he got up and went to the bathroom. After taking a longer-than- usual shower, he emerged from the bathroom to find Layla awake. Staring quietly at the ceiling, she looked like an innocent child.\par
He hesitated for a moment, then walked over to her. Just as he expected, once she saw him, a look of dread spread across her face.\par
\ldblquote D-don\rquote t,\rdblquote  she said, recoiling as he lifted the covers from her body. Ignoring her reluctance, he proceeded to remove her pajamas.\par
As he sponged down her sweaty body, she flailed her arms weakly in an attempt to resist. Once she realized that she was powerless to stop him, she hid her face in her hands.\par
It wasn\rquote t that there were no women available to wash Layla\rquote s body. There were plenty of capable nurses at the military hospital. Matthias just didn\rquote t want to use them. He bristled at the thought of someone else seeing or touching her body, even a woman.\par
\par
He carefully washed her gaunt, trembling body. His eyes twitched briefly when he touched her pregnant belly.\par
Even as the rest of her body had become more and more emaciated, the fetus had continued to grow. He had hoped that she would have a miscarriage, so that she could be given proper treatment, but the fetus had stuck it out.\par
Observing the way it was stubbornly persisting in draining the life out of its mother, he was sure that the baby was his. As he reflected on the fact that Layla, who hated him so much, loved the baby enough that she was willing to sacrifice her own life for it, he was filled with jealousy. He shook his head, realizing how much he bitterly envied his own child. He felt the same hateful envy towards Kyle Ettman, and towards the late Bill Remmer. And not only them. He even felt the same way towards the birds, flowers, and trees that Layla loved. These thoughts made him feel miserable and pathetic, but he couldn\rquote t stop thinking them.\par
Should I get rid of all of them ? he thought to himself. He knew it was an absurd idea, but it brought him some small relief to fantasize about ridding the world of everyone and everything Layla loved. With those distractions out of the way, perhaps she would finally be able to love him. It was a wild, outlandish idea, and yet he knew he was capable of carrying it out.\par
This led him to another realization: that at some point, his love was going to cause Layla to die. That was just how his love worked. He didn\rquote t know any other way to love her, and he didn\rquote t think he ever would.\par
He snapped out of his disturbed reflections and set aside the washcloth. A sullen look hung on his face as he dressed her in a new set of pajamas and pulled the covers back over her.\par
Brushing her hair from her forehead and cheeks, he whispered, \ldblquote Go to sleep.\rdblquote\par
\par
Holding her breath, she nodded and shut her eyes. He could tell she wasn\rquote t sleeping. She just had her eyes closed to avoid him. He knew that she feared and hated him. Nonetheless, he enjoyed gazing at her, so he stayed by her side.\par
\par
