There was a congenial atmosphere at the meeting held at the Sixth Infantry\rquote s headquarters. After years of preparation, the military\rquote s plans were proceeding without a hitch, and the key goal of occupying Sienne had been achieved ahead of schedule. This seemed to definitively refute the cowards who had argued for avoiding war at all costs.\par
\ldblquote With the Fourth Infantry advancing towards the western front, we have decided to keep the Sixth Infantry here in the occupied area for the time being, where it will function as a reserve force, maintaining the stability of the rear supply line,\rdblquote  General von Delman announced, standing in front of a map. The lanky, monocled man was in his sixties, but looked as hale and hearty as\par
\par
someone many years younger. \ldblquote Furthermore, it\rquote s important that we quash those troublesome guerilla fighters as swiftly as possible.\rdblquote  His eyes narrowed as he used his pointer to trace the supply route extending from Berg to the southern end of Lovita.\par
Lovitans in the occupied area had been opposing the Berg military by blocking roads and cutting telephone lines. Occasionally they had gone as far as to attack supply vehicles or snipe at Berg troops, which had especially raised the general\rquote s ire.\par
\ldblquote We have simply been too lax in our management of the occupied area,\rdblquote  General von Delman muttered regretfully. He had consistently expressed the need to manage the occupied area by driving fear into the hearts of its inhabitants. The emperor and crown prince, however, had not been willing to retract their strict order requiring the military to respect the rules laid down in the international treaty.\par
Matthias sat upright in his chair, listening carefully as the general told of the upcoming plans and strategies. Barring a drastic change in the battlefield situation, it seemed that he would remain stationed in Sienne for at least the next few weeks.\par
During that time, he needed to send Layla away. Thinking about this, his breathing faltered momentarily, but he quickly regained his composure.\par
The general scanned the faces of all the commanding officers present, before fixing his gaze on Matthias. \ldblquote Major Herhardt. As you know, the guerillas, protected by international treaty, have severed our communications. We thus need a messenger to report to the rear about our situation on this front. I believe that you will be most fitting for the job. After all, you know the emperor and crown prince well.\rdblquote  The look on the general\rquote s face showed that he\par
\par
had already made up his mind about this, and wasn\rquote t interested in Matthias\rquote s opinion on the matter.\par
The crown prince was commanding the Eighth Infantry in the rear. He was notorious for being hard to deal with, so the choice of messenger was very important.\par
\ldblquote Yes, General,\rdblquote  Matthias replied, accepting the assignment without hesitation.\par
He was sure that Layla would benefit from having him gone for a while. He himself would benefit from it as well, perhaps even more than she would.\par
The general looked relieved at Matthias\rquote s cool-headed acceptance of the assignment. Having heard rumors about the major\rquote s strange behavior, he had worried about his mental stability, but it appeared that his worries were unfounded. He decided to scrap his plan to lecture about the importance of dignified behavior in aristocratic officers, and instead wrapped up the meeting a little early. It seemed that the young officers had the enemy forces to thank for this, specifically the bothersome guerilla fighters.\par
\ldblquote The situation is quite urgent, so we should depart as soon as possible,\rdblquote  the general said with his hand to his throbbing brow. With that, he left the conference room.\par
Matthias put on his officer\rquote s cap and stood up. After adjusting his already- immaculate uniform, he walked out of the building into the dazzling sunlight. It was another bright, sunny day. He snickered at himself for making such an obvious observation, but he didn\rquote t lose his soldierly bearing even for a moment.\par
A driver rushed over to him and said, \ldblquote We\rquote re ready to depart, Major.\rdblquote\par
Matthias nodded and walked towards where the military vehicles were waiting. His car departed from the square at once.\par
