The unfamiliar child was still wandering through the forest, crying. He was now close enough for his sobbing to be audible from the cottage.\par
Is he waiting for his mother? Layla wondered as she sat on a chair on the porch. She was very familiar with the sadness that came from one\rquote s mother never returning. If that was the child\rquote s situation, she felt very sorry for him.\par
Even so, she hesitated to go find him again. She wasn\rquote t sure why, but she felt like she shouldn\rquote t approach him.\par
\ldblquote Uncle Bill, Uncle Bill!\rdblquote  she shouted, hurrying towards Bill, who had just returned to the cottage, pushing a wheelbarrow. The skirt of her school uniform flapped in the breeze as she walked. \ldblquote There\rquote s a child in the woods,\rdblquote  she said.\par
\ldblquote I know. I heard him,\rdblquote  Bill replied as he started to lift his dirt-covered tools out of the wheelbarrow.\par
\ldblquote He keeps crying\'85 What should we do?\rdblquote  she asked, rubbing her hands together nervously. \ldblquote I think he\rquote s waiting for his mother, but he\rquote s been waiting for quite a long time.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Then why don\rquote t you go find him and see how he is?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Me?\rdblquote  she asked, startled by the suggestion. \ldblquote But\'85 I don\rquote t know him.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Strange that you would say that,\rdblquote  he replied, laughing heartily. \ldblquote Layla, do\par
you really not recognize that kid? I\rquote m pretty sure I know who he is\'85\rdblquote  \ldblquote Really? Who is it?\rdblquote\par
\par
Well\'85\rdblquote  he replied, smiling mischievously. Why don\rquote t you go find him? You\rquote ll recognize him once you see him.\rdblquote  With that, he carried his tools with him into the shed.\par
She thought about following after him, but then changed her mind and started walking in the direction of the boy\rquote s cries.\par
When she found the boy, he was crouched on the side of the forest path, whimpering, while birds sang all around the summer woods.\par
After hesitating for a moment, she greeted him warmly, saying, \ldblquote Hi there, little boy.\rdblquote\par
He lifted his head and looked at her. His moist, blue eyes shone like gems. \ldblquote What are you doing here alone?\rdblquote  she asked, taking a cautious step towards\par
him. \ldblquote Where\rquote s your mom?\rdblquote\par
Who is he? she wondered. The more she thought about it, the more perplexed she became, until suddenly she froze. Her heart started pounding as she turned and looked back at the cottage.\par
Yesterday had been identical to today, and she could sense that tomorrow would be the same as well.\par
An eerie feeling spread through her body as she realized that she was stuck at the beginning of summer. The flower buds were not blooming, and the eggs in the waterfowls\rquote  nests remained unhatched. The same was true of the mansion, where everyone was just as busy as ever preparing for Duke Herhardt\rquote s return from service on the overseas war front.\par
She had wanted to live forever in this pleasant time during the early summer of her eighteenth year. Thus, she shouldn\rquote t have known the child. After looking back a few more times, she faced him again.\par
I need to go home, she thought. I\rquote ve got to hurry up and make dinner, because Kyle is coming over to study for the test with me.\par
\par
As she continued to look at the child, however, she started to feel like she recognized him. More precisely, she felt like she recognized who he would be in about twenty years. He looked just like a young version of the man she hated most in the world, while also loving him as much as she hated him.\par
She hesitantly approached the child, gradually increasing her pace with each step.\par
Still crying, he started running towards her as well. Opening her arms widely, she took him into her embrace. The forest breeze enveloped them.\par
When she opened her eyes again, she found herself on her bicycle, riding down the road towards Arvis. She turned the corner and saw the verdant trees of Platanus Road laid out before her. There was a tall man in a Berg officer\rquote s uniform sauntering down the road ahead of her.\par
As the sound of her bicycle\rquote s chain mingled with the sound of the breeze shaking the leaves, she brushed past him.\par
Even though she felt like she shouldn\rquote t, she found herself compelled to look back at him. As soon as she did, the two of them made eye contact. The sound of her heart pounding in her chest grew so loud that it drowned out all other sounds. Just then, she lost her balance and fell.\par
She felt grateful that the wheels of her overturned bicycle were spinning noisily. If it hadn\rquote t been for that, the man might have heard her pounding heart.\par
He approached the spot where she had fallen. She suddenly realized that this had been the start of everything. That beautiful, early-summer day.\par
She then slowly opened her eyes. Looking down at her round belly, she rubbed it. The baby moved gently in response.\par
I\rquote m sorry, she repeated several times as she sat up in bed. I\rquote m so sorry I didn\rquote t recognize you.\par
\par
As she caught her breath and raised her head, her heart was racing, just like on that early-summer day. Her heart always raced when she was with him. She had always assumed this was because he made her frightened and uneasy. Now she realized that there was another reason.\par
The door opened. \ldblquote Layla! You\rquote re awake!\rdblquote  Kyle shouted, rushing towards her with a bright smile on his face. Sitting down by her bedside, he said, \ldblquote I\rquote m glad you\rquote re up. I\rquote ve got something to show you.\rdblquote\par
She looked blankly at the paper in his hands. It was a document bearing the duke\rquote s signature.\par
