The car lurched to a stop as its wheels sank into another mud pit. This had happened countless times already.\par
With a look of resignation on his face, the driver said, \ldblquote I\rquote m sorry, Major.\rdblquote  Matthias nodded to the driver and stepped out of the car.\par
Excessive rains over the previous two days had made a mess of the dirt roads on which they were traveling. However, the paved roads and bridges that might have served as an alternative route had all been destroyed by Lovitan militia forces, so they had no other choice.\par
\ldblquote The sun is about to set. I don\rquote t think it will be safe to travel at night,\rdblquote  said the lieutenant who had come along on the mission, looking with concern at the reddening western horizon.\par
\ldblquote Fine,\rdblquote  Matthias replied. \ldblquote Our unit\rquote s camp is located about an hour from here, so let\rquote s sleep there tonight.\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote Yes, Major, I\rquote ll let the other men know,\rdblquote  the lieutenant replied, looking relieved. He then ran towards the two soldiers who had just managed to push the car out of the mud and informed them of the change of plans.\par
Matthias checked his watch and then lit a cigarette. Even if he wasn\rquote t going to be able to be with Layla anymore, he didn\rquote t want to waste time in this matter. And yet here they were. A bitter smile appeared on his face as he realized how nervous he was. She still has this effect on me, he thought. Maybe she always will. Perhaps I\rquote d better send her off earlier than planned.\par
The front was stable where they were. Even if guerilla forces caused trouble for them, the stability of the troops and supply lines in the rear meant that they should have no problem advancing. However, the crown prince had expressed concern over the unusual quietness of the Southern Alliance forces. Matthias felt the same.\par
The Southern Alliance had fought back during the early stages of the offensive, but now they were allowing Berg to take key strategic points with a suspicious level of passivity. This could be a sign that Berg and its allies had achieved an overwhelming victory, but it could also be a sign of an intentional, strategic retreat by the Southern Alliance.\par
What if, now that Berg had advanced deep into Lo vita, troops from other Southern Alliance nations were planning to enter Lovita and block off Berg\rquote s retreat and supply routes?\par
The crown prince had expressed his concern about this worst-case scenario as well. Of course, General von Delman was certain that none of the countries in the Southern Alliance had any troops left to provide support, and so he would likely insist on continuing with the advance.\par
The key to the whole matter was held by the overseas kingdom of Ettarre. It was Lovita\rquote s most trusted ally, but it had been reluctant to join the war,\par
\par
making no commitment even after several months.\par
It would be nice for Berg if Ettarre continued to betray Lovita like this, but one could never be too sure, as Lovita would likely put a great deal of pressure on their ally to join the fight. If they did end up joining and launched a counterattack against Berg, Sienne would turn into the fiercest battlefield of the war. He needed to get Layla to Berg before that happened.\par
\ldblquote We\rquote re ready to go, Major,\rdblquote  the soldiers shouted.\par
Matthias threw his cigarette to the ground and started walking back to the car. Suddenly, then, he stopped in his tracks, and started looking around with narrowed eyes.\par
\ldblquote Major, is something wr-\rdblquote\par
Matthias raised his hand to stop the soldiers from approaching him. His other hand was already on his holster. Sensing the strange atmosphere, the other men hurried to prepare for battle. Just then, militia forces that had been hiding in the forest launched a surprise attack.\par
Gunshots rang out over the quiet country road.\par
