He checked his body and discovered that the left half of his uniform was soaked with blood. It seemed that he had been hit in the shoulder blade by shrapnel from a mortar blast. The heaviness of the bleeding suggested that it was quite a severe wound, but oddly, he didn\rquote t feel any pain. He simply chuckled hoarsely at the sight of the blood. This wasn\rquote t a good sign.\par
After remaining on the floor looking up at the bright sky for a while longer, he tried to stand up. However, he couldn\rquote t muster the strength. He staggered partway up a few times, each time collapsing back to the floor. His breathing gradually grew more labored, and soon he could detect the rusty scent of blood in his breath.\par
Leaning against the railing, he tried to maintain consciousness. He firmly clutched his head and told himself, Stand up! However, it was no use. Meanwhile, his eyelids started to feel very heavy.\par
The earsplitting artillery\rquote  fire nearby seemed to recede into the distance, becoming faint and muffled.\par
He felt like taking a short nap. He thought that if he could just get some rest, he would then be able to stand up and walk again.\par
Layla\'85 I\rquote ll make it back to you\'85 Just like I promised\'85\par
Just as his eyes were almost completely closed, he noticed the end of a ribbon flapping in the breeze in front of him. It was the hair ribbon that Layla had tied onto his wrist before leaving. Since then, it had become something of a lucky charm for him.\par
He chuckled as he recalled the sight of her wandering through the forest and riverside of Arvis, looking for migratory birds and tying colored string to\par
\par
their legs. When he thought about how much she had longed for their return, and how joyful she was when they finally did come back, it struck him that she had been a very lonely child.\par
Looking blankly at the ribbon flapping in the breeze, he thought about her life, and how full it was with people who never returned, no matter how long she waited for them. Finally, he understood why she loved Arvis\rquote s migratory birds.\par
Layla\'85 he silently mouthed, unable to make a sound anymore.\par
He had promised that he would return to her. She had told him she trusted that he would, that she would always trust him. Thus he had to return to her.\par
Biting his lip, he stood up. Even as his consciousness was growing hazy, the sound of artillery fire remained loud and clear.\par
He began to stagger along on top of the remains of the rampart, which had been reduced to a dusty pile of rubble. He could barely walk straight, but he didn\rquote t stop moving. However, he only made it a few more steps before he fell down again.\par
He pressed his blood-soaked hands against the floor to support his body. Hoarse laughter continued to flow from his lips. He re-opened his tightly shut eyes, and found that his vision had become a bit clearer again. Tears and sweat collected at the end of his chin and then dripped onto the back of his hands. First one drop, then another.\par
He kept blinking to try to regain his focus. Each time he did, the tears dripping onto his hands grew thicker. This made him chuckle even more. The smell of blood in his breath was growing stronger.\par
I must survive, he whispered, just as he had been whispering Layla\rquote s name before. I must survive for you, Layla. My Layla, you \rquote re waiting for me to come back to you, so I must survive.\par
\par
It took even longer for him to muster the strength to stand up this time, but eventually he managed to get to his feet. Wiping his hands across his face, he straightened his posture and started plodding forward. His bloodshot eyes were still half-closed, but he didn\rquote t let that slow him down.\par
He had to keep going. He didn\rquote t want to become yet another longed-for thing in that lonely woman\rquote s life that would never return to her. He wanted to keep his promise, so that she would forgive him. And then he wanted to start the new life with her that she had described, full of love and nothing else.\par
Soon he caught sight up ahead of stairs leading down from the rampart. He quickened his pace. Just as he stepped onto the first stair, a mortar shell struck nearby, causing the section of the rampart where he was to begin to collapse.\par
As the foundation underneath him started to give way, he saw those bright, golden wings fluttering in front of him. This was his last memory;\par
Layla\'85 he whispered, smiling.\par
