\par
family asked for his speedy return, Berg had placed certain conditions on the deal. It was this gentleman\rquote s burdensome task to come check on whether these conditions had been met. He had been selected for the task because he was the cousin of Katharina von Herhardt.\par
\ldblquote We have already confirmed his identity through the means available to us, but we wanted a relative to give final confirmation. That is why we requested your presence,\rdblquote  the officer explained as he guided the gentleman through the quiet ward. \ldblquote He\rquote s in here.\rdblquote\par
They stopped in front of a room at the end of the hallway. The gentleman nodded, and the officer knocked on the door and then opened it.\par
Inside the room, a young man was sitting by the window, smoking a cigarette. He stood up to greet the visitor. A breeze blew through the open window, shaking the tails of his untucked shirt. Because he was standing in front of the bright window, it was hard to make out his face, but it was clear that he was a tall, strong young man.\par
The gentleman clicked his tongue as he observed that the man didn\rquote t look sick or injured at all.\par
He had heard that the man was an officer who had been found severely injured amid the ruins of a battle in which the. Berg soldiers had been thoroughly defeated. He was an enemy soldier, but to the medic who found him, he was simply a person who had survived the battle. He had thus been transported to the rear military hospital for treatment.\par
He was in critical condition, and it took quite a long time before he recovered enough to regain consciousness. Eventually, however, he had made a miraculous recovery. Then, rather than being sent to a prison camp, he had been sent to a very nice hospital. This was because of his status as a high- ranking aristocrat.\par
\par
The Herhardt family had been ready to hold the funeral as soon as the war ended and they recovered the body. It seemed that this man had been playing a trick on everyone. The gentleman wondered what possible reason someone could have for doing nothing when he surely knew that everyone back home thought he was dead. Even considering the chaos of war, the gentleman felt that the doctors who believed this fraudster\rquote s words should be dealt with strictly by the military courts.\par
Just as the gentleman s anger was starting to rise, the young man tossed his cigarette into the ashtray and slowly walked towards the gentleman. The gentleman gasped as he observed that the young man\rquote s elegant posture and movements perfectly matched those of the duke. The young man gazed calmly at him, with a slight smile on his lips, and then nodded slightly to greet him.\par
Taken aback, the gentleman could only chuckle in response.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
147.\par
\par
The Color of My Happiness.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
With the war over, the tight restrictions on border crossing had been loosened. The car passed through the border checkpoint without even\par
an inspection, and then proceeded onto the road leading into Berg. The Berg countryside, which had seen no battles during the war, was incredibly peaceful and serene. It was as if nothing had happened but the changing of the seasons.\par
Matthias gazed calmly out the window at the verdant scenery flowing by.\par
He remained silent for the entire long trip to Ratse.\par
After losing consciousness as the rampart beneath him caved in, he had awoken in bed in a Lovitan military hospital. As he felt the horrible pain throughout his body, he realized that he had survived.\par
I\rquote m alive, he thought. I can return to Layla.\par
This wonderful thought brought him far more relief than the painkillers coursing through his veins. Despite his pain, he laughed with joy. Then, as the\par
\par
medicine took effect and he fell asleep, he dreamed of Layla. It was beautiful.\par
After that, his pain gradually lessened, but he remained bedridden for quite some time. By the time he was able to move around freely, the spring flowers were in full bloom.\par
He was well aware that Berg newspapers had likely been reported him as missing or dead. He was also aware that, even with the war going on, it would have been possible to send a message alerting those in Berg that he was alive. However, he instead decided to wait it out, and he let two seasons pass without sending any messages to Berg.\par
It wasn\rquote t hard for him to make it through that time, because by waiting like that, he was laying the groundwork so that he could openly live with Layla.\par
Of course, he knew it would be hard on Layla, and on his family, all of whom would be needlessly grieving over his supposed death. However, like all decisions he had made with regards to Layla, he did not regret it. Nor would he ever regret it.\par
\ldblquote We\rquote re almost there, Your Grace,\rdblquote  the chauffeur said, joyfully, as he turned onto the boulevard where the museums were located.\par
In contrast to his assistant and the chauffeur, whose eyes were filled with tears, Matthias had a calm, emotionless expression on his face. He nodded, and adjusted his clothes, even though they were already perfect. Then he sat up straighter in his seat and looked down the boulevard at the residential area up ahead.\par
It wouldn\rquote t be long now, but it wasn\rquote t quite time yet. He had some tasks to complete first, but they wouldn\rquote t take too long. The Herhardt family\rquote s Ratse mansion came into sight. A faint smile spread over his face.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
Elise von Herhardt remained skeptical until the end. She understood that the chances were very low that it was a mistake, but she still hesitated to believe. After all, she had already accepted that her son was dead, and had been fully prepared to hold the funeral once his body was recovered. Now she was being told that he was coming home alive!\par
If this turned out to be a lie, she didn\rquote t think she would be able to handle it. This was why she insisted that the matter be kept secret to the outside world. She wasn\rquote t going to believe anything until she saw it with her own eyes.\par
Hurrying into the drawing room, Hessen said, \ldblquote The car has just arrived.\rdblquote\par
Elise and Katharina looked at each other with expressions full of nervous anticipation. The staff members in the room began to stir, and soon the slow, regular rhythm of someone\rquote s footsteps could be heard approaching the room. As Elise watched the door of the room with bated breath, tears were already forming in her eyes. Dowager Herhardt was no different.\par
Both of them recognized those footsteps at once. It could be no one else but Matthias. Sure enough, after a short while, the door slowly opened, and Matthias miraculously appeared.\par
Katharina and Elise both burst into tears at once. Matthias slowly approached them, then came to a stop in the middle of the room, where he was illuminated by a square of sunlight streaming in through the window.\par
With the same calm, respectful elegance that he had displayed while bidding them farewell before he went off to the war, he greeted them. \ldblquote Hello,\par
\par
Grandmother. And hello, Mother. I\rquote ve returned.\rdblquote\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
Layla put on her wide-brimmed straw hat and tied its ribbon under her chin. She then grabbed the blanket and the snacks she had prepared and stuffed them into her bag. Once she put the strap of the bulging bag over her shoulder, she was ready to head out for the picnic. Her French braid bounced behind her as she stepped out of the house with the baby in her arms.\par
As if relieved that he was no longer in the cramped space of his mother\rquote s belly, Felix was growing at an impressive rate. Layla had checked and discovered that he was bigger than the average baby his age. It delighted her to think that he was probably going to grow up to be tall like his father, even if that meant that Felix would quickly become too heavy for her to lift him.\par
After putting him into the baby carriage, she started pushing it and walking towards the park. It was a typical Berg summer day\emdash the sun was bright but not too hot, and there was a refreshing breeze blowing.\par
The baby seemed to be in a particularly good mood, and was babbling excitedly. As they progressed towards the park, Layla would point things out, saying, \ldblquote Look at that!\rdblquote  In response, Felix would giggle delightedly and waggle his little arms and legs.\par
Was Matthias like this as a baby? she wondered. However, it was impossible to imagine Matthias von Herhardt behaving with this much innocent joviality, even as a baby. Rather, she imagined that even when he was Felix\rquote s age, Matthias showed just as much aristocratic reserve as he did now. This thought\par
\par
made her giggle, which caused Felix to giggle as well. Just then, they arrived at the park.\par
It was a weekend, and the park was bustling with people enjoying the nice weather. As she surveyed the crowd, Layla pouted briefly at the sight of all the young couples with their babies, but then she quickly recovered her smile. Seeing his mother smile, Felix then smiled too. That was all she needed.\par
Adjusting the strap of her bag, she pushed the carriage towards a quiet, pretty spot by the forest. As they passed by the fountain at the center of the park, she bought a colorful balloon from a man at a kiosk. She tied the balloon\rquote s string to the carriage, and Felix\rquote s blue eyes widened in amazement.\par
Blue was no longer the color of sadness to Layla. Now it was the color of the greatest happiness she had ever known. This had been the case ever since Felix had first opened his eyes. Or rather, it had been the case since the first morning that she and Matthias had spent together, when they had lain in bed, quietly gazing into each other\rquote s eyes. Or perhaps it had been since even earlier than that. The more she thought about it, the less certain she was of when the shift had occurred.\par
\ldblquote Wow! The balloon is so pretty! Don\rquote t you think so?\rdblquote  she exclaimed to Felix, as he gleefully followed the balloon\rquote s movements with his eyes while giggling and babbling.\par
As the balloon continued to be tossed around by the gentle breeze, the carriage it was attached to came to a stop amid the shade of the park\rquote s forest.\par
\par
The sound of the baby\rquote s laughter mingled with that of his mother, who looked at home amid the greenery of the forest.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
Matthias, the masterpiece of the House of Herhardt, had miraculously returned, and he looked just the same as before he had left. Despite looking the same, however, he said something completely unexpected.\par
\ldblquote Layla is here.\rdblquote\par
He said this in such a calm tone, his mother doubted her ears.\par
He had just finished giving his account of the injuries he had suffered on the front line of the war, followed by his time in the Lovitan hospital and his return to Berg. He had also apologized for the concern he had caused by neglecting his responsibilities. Elise had been surprised, but also relieved, to see him looking the same as always after returning from the brink of death.\par
Now that he was speaking about Layla, however, she was utterly shocked.\par
Not knowing what to say, she turned to her mother-in-law.\par
While the two women exchanged flustered looks, Matthias continued calmly talking.\par
\ldblquote My child is here too.\rdblquote\par
Elise and Katharina, having barely regained their composure, reacted with even more shock than before.\par
\ldblquote Goodness!\rdblquote  Elise exclaimed, almost falling from her seat.\par
Katharina froze with astonishment. While they had already guessed that Matthias and Layla had been in a relationship together, they had never imagined that there was a baby.\par
\par
\ldblquote Layla and I are going to be married,\rdblquote  he said, remaining just as calm and nonchalant as he had been since his arrival.\par
\ldblquote Matthias!\rdblquote  Elise shouted.\par
Katharina let out an exasperated sigh and slumped in her chair. \ldblquote An illegitimate child?\rdblquote  Elise asked.\par
\ldblquote That\rquote s certainly a cause for concern. But there are many other ways to take care of this situation.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Your mother is right, Matthias. Since your engagement to Lady Claudine has been cut off, there are plenty of other ladies from distinguished families to consider, so\'85\rdblquote  Katharina trailed off, clutching her aching head.\par
After thinking for a while longer, it suddenly struck her why Matthias had stayed alone in a hospital in an enemy country for all that time, when he could have easily contacted his Lovitan relatives. By allowing the news of his supposed death to spread throughout Berg, he had gotten something very valuable for himself. Elise seemed to have made the same realization. The two women exchanged knowing glances.\par
\ldblquote I know this is hard for both of you to accept,\rdblquote  Matthias continued. He was paying no mind to the looks of suspicion on the women\rquote s faces. In fact, he was behaving in much the same way as he had when the two women had first suspected he was in a relationship with Layla. \ldblquote However, the world is changing at an ever-increasing rate. Things aren\rquote t the way they used to be.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Matthias\'85\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote To be honest, it doesn\rquote t matter whether the world changes or not.\rdblquote\par
He didn\rquote t seem to be trying very hard to persuade them. Rather, he sounded merely like he was informing them of decisions he had already made.\par
\ldblquote If you really can\rquote t accept it, then I won\rquote t stubbornly insist on burdening the Herhardt name with these troubles.\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote What do you mean by that?\rdblquote  Elise asked, barely able to muster her voice. \ldblquote I will no longer live as a Herhardt.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Matthias!\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Henceforth, the name \lquote Duke Matthias von Herhardt\rquote  will refer to the honorable nobleman who died in battle while fighting for the empire.\rdblquote\par
Goodness. Gone was the joy that Lady Herhardt had felt at seeing Matthias looking like his old self. Perhaps she didn\rquote t know all the ways in which the world was changing, but one thing was clear: the war had completely changed her son.\par
He was obviously not making an empty threat. After living a perfect life with the singular aim of becoming the perfect successor so that he could carry the mantle as the next Duke Herhardt, he now seemed determined to follow a different path entirely. And Elise knew her son well enough to realize that he would do whatever was necessary to follow this new path.\par
Her son had seemingly come back from the dead. And now, she and her mother-in-law had two choices. They could lose him again, or they could accept the things he was telling them.\par
\ldblquote This is the decision I have made,\rdblquote  he said, looking at both of the women. \ldblquote I will not be changing my mind.\rdblquote\par
Elise and Katharina glanced at each other again, then laughed awkwardly.\par
Unshaken, Matthias said, \ldblquote The two of you need to make your choice. I will respect whichever option you prefer.\rdblquote\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
148.\par
\par
The Bird Returned.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
The park in the center of Ratse was large enough to contain a lake and a forest within it. Layla\rquote s favorite part of the park was a spot on its western\par
side that had the prettiest view of the lake and the forest. When she sat there, she almost felt like she was back at Arvis. Despite all the pain and sadness that she had suffered at Arvis, she still missed it dearly, and thought it was the most beautiful place in the world.\par
After spreading out the blanket in the shade of a tree, she took the container of snacks out of her bag. Even though it was just her and the baby, she was determined to have a perfect, pretty picnic. Perhaps Felix was still too young to be able to consciously register the memory, but she figured that the experience would inscribe itself in his heart one way or another.\par
There was a lot that she wanted to share with Felix\emdash moments of happiness with his mother, even trivial ones; affectionate talk; warm looks;\par
\par
laughter. She wanted him to know that no matter what happened, there was someone in the world who would love and believe in him. Uncle Bill had been such a person to her, and she wanted to be sure that Felix would see her the same way.\par
Until the duke came back, she intended to shower Felix with a double dose of love and affection. She would make sure that he always had plenty to eat, and plenty to laugh about, so that he would grow up happy and healthy. Even if he didn\rquote t have a father right now, he was going to get all the love he deserved.\par
After feeding Felix, she gave him his rattle, which he became engrossed in playing with. This gave her the freedom to enjoy her own lunch at a leisurely pace.\par
Just as she was biting into the peach she had brought as dessert, the baby crawled towards her. With each juicy bite she took of the fruit, he bobbled his head in fascination.\par
As she caught a glimpse of his blue eyes staring at her, she froze. He resembled his father so much, she couldn\rquote t stop memories of Matthias from flooding into her mind.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote ll return to you. I promise.\rdblquote\par
Her memory of him promising to return was just as vivid as ever. \ldblquote I love you. \rdblquote\par
She hadn\rquote t gotten the chance to reply to him when he told her he loved her. She took her glasses off and rubbed her eyes, and then looked at the baby and whispered, \ldblquote I love you. I really love you.\rdblquote  She could say this to her heart\rquote s content now. It was the most passionate feeling she had. \ldblquote I love you. I love you,\par
I love you, I love you!\rdblquote\par
Felix seemed to like hearing it. As she kept repeating the phrase, his blue eyes twinkled, and he giggled delightedly.\par
\par
After thoroughly wiping the peach juice from her hands, she lifted the baby into her arms and closed her eyes. The baby\rquote s soft scent tickled her nose. She hugged him tighter.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
The woman who lived next door to Layla was on her way back from a stroll around the neighborhood when she stopped suddenly at the sight of a man knocking at Layla\rquote s door. \ldblquote Excuse me, sir\'85?\rdblquote\par
The man stopped knocking and looked in her direction.\par
\ldblquote Are you looking for the young lady who lives at this house with her baby?\rdblquote  she asked, with an inquisitive look on her face.\par
\ldblquote Yes, I am,\rdblquote  he replied.\par
\ldblquote The young mother with blonde hair and glasses, right?\rdblquote  she asked. \ldblquote Yes,\rdblquote  he replied.\par
After looking closely at his face for a moment, she gasped and said, \ldblquote Oh my. Let me guess\emdash you\rquote re the baby\rquote s father, and you\rquote re just now getting back from the war. Is that right? You look just like the baby. I know we haven\rquote t met before, so I\rquote m sorry for acting so informal. There was just something so familiar about your face from the moment I saw you.\rdblquote  Before he could respond, she continued. \ldblquote Oh but how do you like that? You came all the way back from the war, and you\rquote ve missed her.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote She isn\rquote t home?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No, she left just a short while ago with the baby. She said they were going to the park to have a picnic,\rdblquote  she said, clicking her tongue ruefully.\par
\par
Her eyes remained fixed on the man\rquote s face. She had noticed that the baby didn\rquote t particularly resemble its mother, so it was no surprise that it looked so much like its father. However, there was something else about the man\rquote s face that seemed extremely familiar to her. It didn\rquote t seem like it just because of the baby. She was certain she didn\rquote t know him. She must have seen him somewhere before. But where?\par
\ldblquote She and the baby go to the park a lot,\rdblquote  she went on. \ldblquote They usually don\rquote t come back home until dusk. You could go try to find them, but who knows, you might miss each other again. Maybe you\rquote d better just come wait for her in my house. Would you like to?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No, that\rquote s all right,\rdblquote  he said, glancing in the direction of the park. He walked down the steps from the front door and stopped in front of the woman and said, \ldblquote Thank you, ma\rquote am.\rdblquote  With that, he brushed past her and went on his way at a relaxed pace.\par
As he reached the end of the road, where a black car was waiting, a man got out of the driver\rquote s seat and hurriedly opened the back passenger door. This piqued the woman\rquote s curiosity even more. She could tell that he wasn\rquote t any ordinary man. She kept watching the car as it drove off into the distance.\par
\ldblquote I know I\rquote ve seen him somewhere before\'85\rdblquote  she muttered, as a gentle breeze blew through the neighborhood on that quiet weekend afternoon.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
After eating and playing to his heart\rquote s content, little Felix fell asleep. For a while, Layla lay next to him on the picnic blanket, gazing at his angelic face.\par
\par
Then, sighing with relief, she sat up. Her hair, which had become Felix\rquote s plaything, was quite disheveled. Her glasses were covered with his tiny fingerprints, making everything she saw through them look blurry. She cautiously inched towards her bag, which was on the edge of the blanket. First she rubbed her glasses clean, then she let down her messy hair.\par
Felix liked her hair as much as his father did. It thus seemed that he took after his father in more ways than just his appearance, although the son was a bit more reckless than the father in terms of how he treated her hair. Whenever she was holding Felix in her arms, he would become transfixed by her shimmering blonde hair and grab at it with both hands, laughing joyously. She always felt sorry when she had to wrest it out of his grip.\par
She never would have thought the day would come when having her hair grabbed made her happy. Chuckling to herself about this, she started combing her fingers through her hair to attempt to tame it. Even when she put it in a ponytail or a braid, Felix always managed to grab it. The only way to prevent that would be by cutting it, but she didn\rquote t want to do that.\par
Her fine blonde hair flowed down over the collar and shoulders of her white dress. Just as she was about to start braiding it, she got distracted by the sight of the late- afternoon sunlight streaming through the forest\rquote s thick foliage. As she gazed at the light, transfixed, the still surface of the lake started glimmering with a rich, golden hue.\par
\ldblquote So pretty\'85\rdblquote  she muttered.\par
The whole environment around her looked as peaceful and beautiful as a painting. The time she had spent in that hellish city, where bombs fell from the sky, now felt like a faintly remembered dream.\par
She pulled her legs towards her chest and rested her cheek on her knees.\par
\par
Some waterfowl slew leisurely over the lake. They were migratory birds that spent their summers in Berg. A breeze brushed past the wild rose bushes in the forest, picking up their sweet scent. The rustling of the lush vegetation reminded her of the sound of her bicycle\rquote s chain.\par
Suddenly, her enjoyment of the perfect, beautiful summer day was interrupted by a strange feeling.\par
How can it be so nice? He\rquote s not here. He hasn\rquote t come back.\par
The strange feeling slowly grew, until the sound of a baby\rquote s crying snapped her back to reality. Felix had awoken, and was staring up at the sky, wailing. She saw the reason at once. The balloon had come untied from the handle of the baby carriage, and it had floated up and gotten caught on a branch of a nearby tree.\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s all right!\rdblquote  she cried out, trying to calm him down. \ldblquote It\rquote s no big deal.\par
Everything will be just fine!\rdblquote\par
Felix stopped crying, stunned by the sudden rise in his mother\rquote s energy level. She wiped the tears from his face, and then bolted up and started heading for the tree where the balloon was stuck.\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t worry, Mommy will get it for you!\rdblquote\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
The park was huge, and all that Matthias had been told was that she was somewhere within it. Nonetheless, it was easy for him to find her. He knew she would be at a spot near the forest and the lake, where there would be a lot of birds. He knew that she would prefer a relatively quiet spot, without too\par
\par
many people around. Then again, it wouldn\rquote t be completely isolated, either\emdash  despite the tough front she put on, he knew his beautiful bird was timid at heart.\par
He headed straight to a spot that fit this description and, sure enough, she was there. Sitting in the shade of the trees on a picnic blanket, she was gazing at the scenery, until her attention was drawn away by a crying baby next to her. It was only then that Matthias realized there was another person there\emdash  his child.\par
He stopped in his tracks to take in the sight. Meanwhile, Layla sprung up and started walking quickly towards the woods. The baby tilted its head with interest as it watched its mother.\par
Matthias then resumed walking towards them. He reached the edge of the blanket just as Layla reached the foot of a tree a short distance away.\par
He looked at her for a moment, wondering what she was up to. Then, all of a sudden, she started climbing up the tree. When he saw the balloon caught on a branch midway up the tree, he finally understood what she was doing.\par
He chuckled to himself. Just as he did, the baby looked up at him. He looked down, and the two of them made eye contact. He raised his eyebrows slightly. The baby blinked in surprise.\par
He laughed once more, a little louder this time. When he looked up again, Layla was already straddling the branch, reaching out for the balloon that was caught on it.\par
Without hesitation, he started walking towards the tree.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
The balloon was caught near the end of the branch, making it hard to reach, but once Layla stretched out her arm as far as she could, she was able to grab it. \ldblquote Look! Mommy found the\'85\rdblquote  She trailed off before she could finish her cheerful exclamation.\par
Near the foot of the tree, there was a man, standing up straight with one arm behind his back, looking up at her. When their eyes met, he cocked his head in a mischievous way that stood in contrast to his dignified bearing.\par
It was Matthias. Her mind had gone completely blank, but she was certain of that. As far-fetched as it sounded, she had no doubt. It was him. It couldn\rquote t be anyone else.\par
Holding the balloon in one hand, she continued to stare at him. Her other hand was trembling slightly as she used it to keep her balance on the tree branch. As he gazed up at her with his gleaming blue eyes, a smile slowly spread across his face.\par
Without saying anything, he moved his left hand from behind his back. The ends of the white ribbon tied to his wrist fluttered in the soft evening breeze.\par
It was strange. She had been ardently missing him, thinking about him several times each day. And yet now, she couldn\rquote t find any words to say to him. All she could do was bite her lip and stare at him with widened eyes. Also remaining silent, he slowly spread out his arms.\par
\par
Her memories after that were hazy. She vaguely recalled nimbly descending the tree and running towards him. The tears flowing from her eyes blurred her vision, but she didn\rquote t care. As she ran faster and faster towards him, her hair billowed behind her like a pair of golden wings. There was so much she wanted to say to him, but when she opened her lips, all that came out was a cry that sounded even more infantile than that of their son.\par
She flew into Matthias\rquote s arms as lightly as a bird. As she did so, the balloon string slipped from her grip, and the balloon started floating away.\par
With eyes that looked just like his father\rquote s, little Felix gazed at the pretty balloon as it ascended far up into the sky. For a moment, he seemed about to cry, but then his eyes went wide with amazement. In the spot in the sky where the balloon had receded out of sight, a flock of birds was flying by.\par
As his eyes started sparkling with fascination, he shifted his gaze to his mother and father, who were embracing each other. For a moment, he seemed perplexed, as if he was wondering if he should start crying like his mother was. Instead, he burst into delighted laughter.\par
As the setting sun infused the sky with a rosy hue, the flock of birds descended and landed on the lake.\par
I knew my bird would return, Layla thought. It was a wonderful summer evening.\par
